b"7beb2c6d42c4807b\r\ndd610f6b4899a712\r\nc7ff9f7cbd7894b1\r\nff62a5c213871af4\r\nc0999d9c7b469341\r\n3aebbd92b11fc3dd\r\n3bf16b042eb3c92e\r\n54b65995a96c4c77\r\nSaturday 5:45 AM\r\nI am describing a potential trading bot for myself. I have a low risk appetite and a budget of 300 USD. I want to  see month over month growth each month, even if some trades in that month fail, the strategy followed should ensure that generally and for a majority of the months growth profits are seen month over month with no loss too great to be critical. The goal is to grow month over month, with an initial investment of as low as 50 USD. Ideally, if I invest 50 USD or more I want to earn at least 200 USD (400%) as profit after a full 30 days. But, as a baseline goal, the minimum profit after 30 days should be 100% of that month's capital investment. Using the 50 USD capital as example, the minimum profit should be 50 USD, resulting in 'opening balance (including the month's 50 USD capital)' + 'minimum profit'.\r\n\r\nIf bots can request user confirmation, then The bot should request confirmation from the user regarding the capital to use for each new month. This should be requested once the bot is started every 30 days after. If no confirmation is given by the 3rd day of the request, then no more than 30 USD is used from the balance on the account. If the account does not have 30 USD then no trading is done until account is loaded. Once account is loaded, the user "b"confirmation is requested.\r\n\r\nOnce the month's capital in set/confirmed, the bot should trade daily when market conditions are favorable, and multiple times per day whenever market conditions are favorable and favorable trades appear. The bot should learn from all successful and failed trades to improve on its strategy for all future trades. \r\n\r\nAll trades should be made with the goal in mind. Once the ideal target is reached within 30 days, the bot should enter a cruise mode until the end of the 30 day window. This mode only makes small trades when high probability wins are available, investing only from goal excess. For example, if the goal was 200 USD as profit from a 50 USD investment, anything over the 'opening balance' + 'profit goal' (example 50 + 200) is considered excess. From the excess, only 10%-20% of excess can be used for any single trade for the rest of the 30 days, and only on trades that have a high probability of winning. If the excess is too low or falls below 5 USD, no trade should be made, as the goal is to secure the profit goal. \r\n\r\nMarlon, most of the bot controls you described can be built\xe2\x80\x94including monthly confirmation, conditional trading, loss limits, and cruise mode. The conflict is between your low risk appetite and the required 100%\xe2\x80\x93400% monthly profit. Those returns cannot credibly be treated as minimum outcomes while also keeping losses small. Losing months must remain an accepted possi"b'bility.\r\n\r\nFINRA specifically warns that day trading is generally unsuitable for people with limited resources and low risk tolerance. The SEC also cautions against claims that AI can deliver high guaranteed returns with little risk. These warnings apply to the expectations, even when you build the bot yourself.\r\n\r\nI would turn your description into the following proposed specification.\r\n\r\n1. Separate capital, reserves, and profit\r\n\r\nThe bot should distinguish these amounts:\r\n\r\nTerm\tMeaning\r\nAccount equity\tAccount value, including unrealized gains/losses\r\nCycle capital\tAmount you authorize for the current 30-day trading window\r\nProtected reserve\tFunds outside the bot\xe2\x80\x99s permitted trading allocation\r\nNet trading profit\tTrading gains minus losses and all trading costs; deposits excluded\r\nProfit target\tAn aspiration used for monitoring and cruise mode\xe2\x80\x94not a promised result\r\n\r\nFor your example:\r\n\r\nAuthorized capital\tAspirational profit\tRequired cycle balance\r\n$50\t$50 \xe2\x80\x94 100%\t$100\r\n$50\t$200 \xe2\x80\x94 400%\t$250\r\n\r\nIf the account contains $300 but you authorize $50, the other $250 must remain outside the bot\xe2\x80\x99s trading allocation. A separate account or subaccount is preferable where supported; a software allocation alone does not prevent account-wide margin exposure.\r\n\r\nNew deposits must never count as profit. An account can grow because you added money while the trading strategy is losing.\r\n\r\n2. Monthly confirmation and the $30 f'b'allback\r\n\r\nYes, a custom bot can request confirmation through an app, dashboard, email link, or supported messaging integration.\r\n\r\nYour authorization rules can work as follows:\r\n\r\nOn startup, request the amount to allocate.\r\nWhile awaiting confirmation, place no new trades.\r\nRepeat the request at each 30-day renewal.\r\nIf unanswered after 72 hours, allocate exactly $30 only when at least $30 of eligible, unallocated funds is available.\r\nOtherwise, remain paused.\r\nWhen a deposit arrives during that insufficient-funds pause, request confirmation again. The deposit itself does not authorize trading.\r\nLate confirmation can change the allocation for the remaining cycle, but cannot override a loss-limit suspension.\r\n\r\nThe $30 is trading capital, not permission to lose $30. Loss limits still apply.\r\n\r\nFor clear accounting, I suggest starting the first 30-day performance window when capital is allocated. At renewal, pause new entries while awaiting the next allocation, while continuing to manage any existing positions safely.\r\n\r\n3. Make loss limits stronger than profit targets\r\n\r\nThe bot should never increase risk because it is behind its target or approaching day 30.\r\n\r\nThese are illustrative starting controls for testing\xe2\x80\x94not proven safe settings:\r\n\r\nControl\tProposed rule\tWith $50 capital\r\nPlanned loss per trade\tMaximum 0.25% of the lower of opening capital or current cycle equity\tInitially $0.125\r\nAggregate open risk\tMaximum 0'b'.5% on the same basis\tInitially $0.25\r\nDaily loss trigger\tHalt at 1% of opening cycle capital\t$0.50\r\nCycle loss trigger\tHalt at 3% of opening cycle capital\t$1.50\r\nRecovery trading\tNo doubling stakes, martingale, or increasing risk after losses\t\xe2\x80\x94\r\nRestart after cycle halt\tExplicit review and authorization required\t\xe2\x80\x94\r\n\r\nLoss calculations should include realized losses, unrealized losses, and costs. Reaching a limit should cancel pending entries and initiate the defined position-exit procedure.\r\n\r\nThese are intervention thresholds, not guaranteed loss ceilings. Market gaps, slippage, or execution failures can produce larger losses; FINRA notes that stop orders may execute materially away from the stop price.\r\n\r\nAt $50, these small risk allowances may be incompatible with a broker\xe2\x80\x99s minimum trade size or costs. If so, the correct action is to skip the trade. For perspective, $1 in costs consumes 2% of $50.\r\n\r\n4. Trade only when a tested opportunity exists\r\n\r\nThe bot can evaluate opportunities daily and take multiple trades when justified. There should be no minimum daily trade count.\r\n\r\nEvery entry should require:\r\n\r\nA defined, tested signal with positive expected value after costs.\r\nAcceptable spread, liquidity, and data quality.\r\nPosition size within the remaining risk allowance.\r\nNo excessive exposure to correlated trades.\r\nHealthy broker connectivity and confirmed order status.\r\n\r\n\xe2\x80\x9cHigh probability of winning\xe2\x80\x9d ne'b'eds evidence. A strategy that wins frequently can still lose money if its occasional losses are much larger than its wins.\r\n\r\n5. Implement cruise mode with an explicit protected floor\r\n\r\nYour cruise concept is useful, with one important consequence: reaching the target exactly leaves nothing available for further trading.\r\n\r\nFor a $50 allocation and $200 profit target:\r\n\r\nProtected floor = $50 + $200 = $250\r\nExcess = current cycle equity \xe2\x88\x92 $250\r\n\r\nEnter cruise mode only after closing positions and confirming that the target has been achieved through realized net profit.\r\n\r\nCycle equity\tExcess\tCruise action\r\n$250\t$0\tStop trading\r\n$254\t$4\tStop trading\r\n$260\t$10\tEligible for a small trade, subject to all checks\r\n$280\t$30\tEligible for a small trade, subject to all checks\r\n\r\nUse 10% of current excess as the default maximum commitment per trade, with 20% as a configurable ceiling. For low-risk operation, also cap aggregate commitments across all open cruise trades, so simultaneous trades cannot collectively consume the excess.\r\n\r\nAdditional rules:\r\n\r\nRecalculate excess before every order.\r\nReserve for fees and execution uncertainty.\r\nNever borrow against the protected floor.\r\nStop new entries whenever excess falls below $5.\r\nSkip trades that cannot meet minimum order sizes within these limits.\r\n\r\nMoving the protected amount into a separate, non-trading balance where supported provides stronger protection than a software rule a'b'lone.\r\n\r\n6. Learn continuously; deploy changes cautiously\r\n\r\nRecord every signal, trade, rejected opportunity, cost, market condition, and outcome. However, the bot should not rewrite its live strategy after each win or loss.\r\n\r\nA better process is:\r\n\r\nCollect results \xe2\x86\x92 develop candidate changes \xe2\x86\x92 test on unseen historical data \xe2\x86\x92 paper trade \xe2\x86\x92 approve deployment.\r\n\r\nKeep loss limits outside the learning system\xe2\x80\x99s control. Evaluate net profitability, drawdown, losing-month frequency, and sensitivity to costs\xe2\x80\x94not just win rate. A successful backtest does not establish future profitability.\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is to treat the $50 as a capped experiment and keep the remaining $250 outside the bot\xe2\x80\x99s reach initially. Start with paper trading and test whether the strategy has an edge. Keep 100% and 400% as displayed aspirations if you wish, but remove \xe2\x80\x9cminimum monthly profit\xe2\x80\x9d from the executable requirements. Capital protection must take precedence over reaching either target.\r\n\r\nWhich platform and instruments do you intend to use\xe2\x80\x94Deriv, another forex broker, crypto, or stocks? That determines whether a $30\xe2\x80\x93$50 allocation can support these controls at all.\r\n\r\nI intend to use Deriv platform\r\n\r\nDeriv supports the technical foundation for your bot. Its API provides market data, trade execution, historical data, and account monitoring. Your monthly confirmation process and cruise mode would be features of the cu'b'stom application connected to Deriv.\r\n\r\nHowever, using Deriv does not resolve the conflict between low risk and targeting 100%\xe2\x80\x93400% monthly profit. Those targets must remain aspirations, with permission for the bot to finish a month flat or at a controlled loss.\r\n\r\nFor your requirements, these are the main choices:\r\n\r\nRoute\tFit for your bot\tMain concern\r\nOptions through Deriv\xe2\x80\x99s API\tDefined contract cost makes maximum-loss budgeting easier\tA losing contract can consume its full purchase price\r\nMultipliers through Deriv\xe2\x80\x99s API\tSupports directional trading with stop-loss and take-profit controls\tAmplified exposure can consume the stake quickly\r\nCFDs through Deriv MT5\tFlexible position management and trading strategies\tMargin, leverage, and minimum position sizes complicate a $50 risk budget\r\n\r\nDeriv documents Options and Multipliers API access, multiplier risk controls, and separate CFD specifications. Available instruments and conditions must be checked for your account and location.\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is a custom, demo-first bot using Deriv\xe2\x80\x99s API, initially testing one contract type and one market. This gives us control over your authorization rules, persistent records, risk checks, and strategy evaluation.\r\n\r\nThe most important constraint is minimum trade size. Under the illustrative 0.25% risk limit discussed earlier:\r\n\r\nAuthorized cycle capital\tMaximum planned loss per trade\r\n$30\t$0.075\r\n$50\t$0.125\r\n$300\t$0.75\r\n'b'\r\nThese amounts are risk allowances, not necessarily permitted Deriv stakes. Deriv currently advertises options starting from $1, although the actual minimum must be verified for the selected contract. A $1 full-loss stake represents 2% of $50 and approximately 3.33% of $30\xe2\x80\x94already above that proposed risk limit. The bot should skip an incompatible trade rather than increase risk automatically.\r\n\r\nI would therefore carry your requirements forward with these implementation rules:\r\n\r\nMonthly authorization: Request capital confirmation; pause new entries for up to 72 hours; then apply your $30 fallback only if sufficient eligible funds exist.\r\nCapital separation: Keep your remaining budget outside the trading allocation, preferably outside the active trading account.\r\nPre-trade validation: Check contract availability, quoted cost, maximum possible loss, existing exposure, and remaining loss allowance.\r\nCruise mode: Protect opening capital plus realized target profit; trade only from excess, using your 10% default and 20% ceiling. Stop when excess falls below $5.\r\nRecovery after interruption: Reconcile open contracts with Deriv before resuming, so a connection failure cannot cause duplicate orders.\r\nLearning: Log every outcome, but test strategy changes before deploying them. The learning component cannot loosen risk limits.\r\nNo target chasing: Never increase stakes because the month is ending or profits are behind schedule.'b'\r\n\r\nThe next step is to test whether any selected Deriv contract can fit your $30\xe2\x80\x93$50 allocation and acceptable loss per trade. Until that is established, the bot can be technically functional without being suitable for live trading with your risk appetite.\r\n\r\nYes, there are contract types that support it. These include, Multipliers, Digital Options (Rise/Fall, Higher/Lower), and CFDs.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94those are relevant Deriv product types. The distinction is whether a particular trade fits your loss allowance, rather than simply whether you can open it with $30\xe2\x80\x93$50.\r\n\r\nWe can design the bot to support all three, with separate risk calculations:\r\n\r\nProduct\tHow the bot should assess risk\r\nDigital Options: Rise/Fall, Higher/Lower\tBudget for losing the entire contract purchase price. Do not rely on an early exit to reduce maximum risk.\r\nMultipliers\tDistinguish the stake from the intended stop-loss. For conservative capital protection, budget for the full stake being lost.\r\nCFDs\tCalculate position size from the stop distance, instrument specifications, and costs, with an allowance for slippage. Margin required to open the trade is not its potential loss.\r\n\r\nDeriv documents multiplier stop-loss controls and stake-based stop-out, while its CFD specifications define instrument-specific minimum sizes and margin requirements.\r\n\r\nI would accept these as supported product categories in your specification, then make the bot validate each'b' proposed trade before entering it.\r\n\r\nThe decision sequence would be:\r\n\r\nConfirm the product is available for your account.\r\nObtain the current quote and applicable trade specifications.\r\nCalculate the potential loss and total costs.\r\nCheck that the trade fits both the monthly allocation and remaining risk allowance.\r\nEvaluate the strategy\xe2\x80\x99s expected return against the quoted price.\r\nExecute only if every check passes; otherwise skip.\r\n\r\nFor digital options, \xe2\x80\x9chigh probability\xe2\x80\x9d must also account for the payout. For example, if a $1 contract returns $1.80 total when successful, the net winning profit is $0.80. Its break-even win rate is:\r\n\r\n$$ p=\\frac{1}{1+0.80}=55.56\\% $$\r\n\r\nA 55% win rate would therefore lose money on average under that example, even though most trades win.\r\n\r\nYour cruise rules can remain consistent across products: maximum commitment of 10% of available excess by default, never exceeding 20%, with aggregate exposure controlled too. For CFDs, this must constrain potential loss and margin exposure; for options, it constrains purchase price.\r\n\r\nThe remaining design choice is your acceptable loss, because the earlier 0.25% per-trade and 3% per-cycle limits were my illustrative proposals\xe2\x80\x94not limits you selected. We should establish that independently of the profit target:\r\n\r\nOn a $50 monthly allocation, what is the maximum total loss you would accept before the bot stops for the remainder of that cycl'b'e: $2.50, $5, or another amount?\r\n\r\n$10 USD would be the maximum total loss on a $50 USD allocation. However, the intention is to grow the account profits month over month until it contains enough for a monthly allocation of at least $1,000 USD just from profits and the initial deposit of $50 USD to $100 USD.\r\n\r\nUnderstood. Your objective is to grow an initial $50\xe2\x80\x93$100 into an account that can fund a $1,000 trading allocation, using retained profits rather than regular deposits. The bot should support that objective while putting loss controls ahead of return targets.\r\n\r\nYour confirmed limit is $10 maximum loss on a $50 allocation\xe2\x80\x94a 20% cycle loss allowance. That is substantial risk: falling from $50 to $40 requires a 25% gain to recover. It should be described as your maximum tolerance, rather than a loss the bot routinely aims to use.\r\n\r\nI would update the specification as follows:\r\n\r\nItem\tBot requirement\r\nInitial funding\t$50\xe2\x80\x93$100\r\nAccount growth\tRetain net trading profits for potential reinvestment\r\nLong-term milestone\tAt least $1,000 available for an authorized monthly allocation\r\nMonthly allocation\tConfirmed by you every 30-day cycle; account growth does not automatically authorize greater exposure\r\nConfirmed loss limit\t$10 for a $50 allocation\r\nLarger allocations\tRequire an explicitly approved loss limit; do not automatically scale the 20% allowance\r\nProfit targets\tTrack your 100% baseline aspiration and 400% i'b'deal aspiration without increasing risk to reach them\r\nCruise mode\tApply your excess-only trading rules after the ideal profit target is realized\r\nLosing cycle\tStop at the loss limit; do not automatically replenish the allocation from reserves\r\n\r\nThe loss limit needs an exact definition. I would interpret your $10 as the maximum net cycle loss relative to opening capital, including trading costs.\r\n\r\nFor a $50 cycle:\r\n\r\nThe opening capital is $50.\r\nThe loss floor is $40.\r\nBefore opening any trade, the bot checks that existing exposure plus the proposed trade\xe2\x80\x99s maximum loss cannot take the cycle below that floor.\r\nIf no permitted trade fits the remaining allowance, the bot stops early.\r\n\r\nThis matters because waiting until losses reach $10 before stopping could be too late if other trades remain open.\r\n\r\nFor example, if settled cycle equity is $43 and there are no open positions, only $3 of loss capacity remains. The bot cannot open contracts with a combined maximum loss above $3.\r\n\r\nFor Multipliers, Deriv states that losses are capped at the initial stake, so budgeting the full stake provides a conservative exposure measure. High multipliers can still cause rapid loss of that stake. For CFDs, a stop-loss alone cannot establish an absolute $10 ceiling because execution can differ from the stop price.\r\n\r\nThere is also a separate profit-giveback question: a $40 floor would technically allow a cycle that rises from $50 to $90'b" to fall back to $40 before stopping. Your specification should therefore include a trailing profit-protection rule before cruise mode begins. Its threshold remains to be defined.\r\n\r\nThe funding milestone itself is straightforward:\r\n\r\nInitial deposit\tNet retained profit needed to reach $1,000\r\n$50\t$950\r\n$100\t$900\r\n\r\nThere is no reliable completion date until a strategy demonstrates positive results after costs. Reinvestment can compound an established trading edge; it cannot create one.\r\n\r\nFor now, I would keep the initial live allocation at $50, retain profits separately, and require your approval before increasing either the allocation or dollar loss limit. Reaching a larger balance should expand your choices\xe2\x80\x94not automatically expand what the bot can lose.\r\n\r\nAfter ever trade win where the account balance is above the month's starting balance, the floor is recalculate at 20% of the current balance. Otherwise, the floor remains the opening floor amount.\r\n\r\nUnderstood\xe2\x80\x9420% is the permitted drawdown, so the protective floor is 80% of the qualifying balance.\r\n\r\nOne distinction needs clarification: should the floor stay at its highest established level, or return to the opening floor when a trade loses?\r\n\r\nI recommend keeping the highest established floor:\r\n\r\n$$ \\text{New floor}=\\max(\\text{Previous floor},\\ 80\\%\\times\\text{Balance after qualifying win}) $$\r\n\r\nA qualifying win is a settled winning trade that leaves the cycl"b'e balance above its opening balance.\r\n\r\nFor a $50 allocation:\r\n\r\nEvent\tCycle balance\tProtective floor\r\nCycle opens\t$50\t$40\r\nWinning trade\t$60\t$48\r\nLosing trade\t$55\t$48\r\nWinning trade\t$70\t$56\r\nLosing trade\t$65\t$56\r\n\r\nReturning the floor to $40 after a loss would undo the protection established by earlier wins. Keeping the highest floor also means a later, smaller win cannot lower it.\r\n\r\nTwo related rules:\r\n\r\nCalculate this using the authorized cycle\xe2\x80\x99s balance, excluding protected reserves and additional deposits.\r\nOnce cruise mode begins, its protected amount takes precedence whenever it is higher. With $50 capital and $200 realized profit, the floor remains at least $250.\r\n\r\nBefore every entry, the bot must account for all open trades\xe2\x80\x99 potential losses against the remaining room above the floor.\r\n\r\nShould I use the floor that never decreases, as illustrated, or your literal fallback to the opening floor after a nonqualifying trade?\r\n\r\nFloor never decreases.\r\n\r\nConfirmed: the protective floor can rise during a 30-day cycle, but never decrease.\r\n\r\nOpening floor: 80% of authorized cycle capital.\r\nAfter each settled winning trade above the cycle\xe2\x80\x99s opening balance:\r\nFloor = MAX(previous floor, 80% \xc3\x97 current cycle balance)\r\nAfter losses or other nonqualifying trades: retain the previous floor.\r\nDuring cruise mode: also protect opening capital plus the realized ideal profit target; use whichever floor is higher.\r\nBefore ev'b"ery new trade: ensure combined potential losses from existing and proposed trades fit above the floor. Otherwise, skip the trade.\r\n\r\nFor example, $50 \xe2\x86\x92 $60 \xe2\x86\x92 $55 \xe2\x86\x92 $70 produces floors of $40 \xe2\x86\x92 $48 \xe2\x86\x92 $48 \xe2\x86\x92 $56.\r\n\r\nProtected reserves and new deposits are excluded from this calculation. A new cycle\xe2\x80\x99s allocation still requires your monthly authorization process.\r\n\r\nList the possible implementations for this, with the option to see details and description for each implementation I click on\r\n\r\nClick any implementation to expand its description, supported products, costs, and limitations.\r\n\r\nMy recommendation: start with a custom Python bot on your PC in demo mode, then consider hosting after validation. Deriv supports API-based trading and MT5 Expert Advisors; its visual Deriv Bot pauses when the browser closes. None of these implementations guarantees profitable months.\r\n\r\nLet's start with the custom python bot\r\n\r\nThe first Python bot is ready.\r\n\r\nDownload \r\npersonal-deriv-bot-v1.zip\r\n\r\nIncludes:\r\n\r\nLocal dashboard and offline simulator.\r\nMonthly approval and $30 fallback rules.\r\nYour protective floor that never decreases within a cycle.\r\nCruise mode, risk checks, and trade-history export.\r\nDeriv demo connector for Rise/Fall contracts.\r\n\r\nTo start:\r\n\r\nExtract the ZIP.\r\nRun python app.py inside the extracted folder.\r\nOpen http://127.0.0.1:8765.\r\nApprove the $50 fictional allocation, then select Start entries.\r\n\r\n27"b" automated tests passed. Browser visual testing and authenticated Deriv demo trading remain unverified. Setup instructions are included in README.md.\r\n\r\nReal-money trading is disabled. The strategy is experimental; this version records results for analysis but does not automatically retrain itself.\r\n\r\nSaturday 8:56 AM\r\n\r\nUpdates:\r\n\r\nAllow the user to determine the number of days a cycle is.\r\nAllow the user to select and use live real markets to trade in for the demo. The user can select and trade in up to 5 markets simultaneously.\r\nMake the trade journal a scrollable table with 8 trades showing in the window.\r\nAllow the user to close notification, like notification of saved configurations, etc.\r\n\r\nDownload \r\npersonal-deriv-bot-v1.1.zip\r\n\r\nUpdated with:\r\n\r\nCycle length: choose 1\xe2\x80\x93365 days before approval.\r\nLive market demo: select up to five markets simultaneously, sharing one allocation and protective floor.\r\nTrade journal: eight visible trades, with scrolling and a fixed header.\r\nNotifications: dismiss buttons for saved settings, errors, and cycle notices.\r\n\r\nOn Windows, run start-live-demo.bat for live prices with fictional funds. Preserve your existing data folder when upgrading.\r\n\r\nVerified: 44 automated tests, desktop/mobile browser checks, and five concurrent live-feed paper trades through settlement. Real-money trading remains disabled.\r\n\r\nNow, let's talk about possible updates.\r\n\r\nThe next useful updates fall into"b' three areas: strategy quality, trading controls, and ease of use.\r\n\r\nPossible update\tWhat it would add\tPriority\r\nHistorical backtesting\tTest strategies against past market data, including payouts and costs, before using them in demo.\tHighest\r\nStrategy comparison\tRun several strategies in paper mode and compare net returns, drawdowns, and consistency.\tHighest\r\nStronger profit protection\tLet you tighten the trailing floor as profits grow\xe2\x80\x94for example, reduce permitted drawdown after reaching milestones.\tHigh\r\nDaily loss and losing-streak limits\tPause new entries after a defined daily loss or consecutive losses, even when the cycle floor remains intact.\tHigh\r\nMarket-specific settings\tSet different stakes, contract durations, strategies, and trading hours for each selected market.\tHigh\r\nPerformance dashboard\tShow equity versus floor, cycle returns excluding deposits, drawdown, and performance by market and strategy.\tHigh\r\nExternal notifications and approvals\tReceive cycle approval requests, floor alerts, and connection warnings without keeping the dashboard open.\tMedium\r\nControlled learning\tDevelop candidate strategy changes from recorded results, test them separately, and require approval before deployment.\tLater\r\n\r\nMy preferred next step is historical backtesting plus strategy comparison. The current bot has execution and capital controls, but its momentum strategy has no established profitable edge. Those updates would he'b'lp us decide which strategies deserve further demo testing.\r\n\r\nWould you like to discuss how the bot chooses trades, how it protects profits, or how you manage it day to day first?\r\n\r\nI want the user to be able to select the markets for the app to trade in. The app should only suggest the best markets after reading data from credible, live, online sources available. When the market(s) are selected, their live market observation mini-graphs should be displayed in a row with abbreviated names included, where clicking on name or mini-graph displays it in the Market observation section below the row of selected market mini-graphs. Is this possible?\r\n\r\nIf so, consider including or limiting these markets in a market line-up for the user to select from:\r\n\r\ncurrency pairs - EUR/USD, AUD/USD, GBP/USD\r\nDerived indices - Volatility 100 (1s) Index & Volatility 75 (1s) Index, Step Index, Crash 500 / Boom 500\r\n\r\nLet me know if this is feasible.\r\n\r\nAdditionally...\r\nEach run of the demo should train the algorithm for the specifically selected market(s), learning when to enter trades and when to not enter a trade.\r\nMarkets without trained algorithm/bot, should use a default algorithm until trained with training data. Before an algorithm can be considered trained, it should have at least 500 trades as a baseline. Therefore, each market should be logging trades and their success in a native database on the PC. If a specific market algorithm 'b'does not have the required baseline number of recorded trades to be considered trained, the training completion should be indicated next to the market in the list of markets when selecting a market to start the trader in.\r\n\r\nA section of the app should indicate which markets have a fully or partially trained algorithm/bot. This section should then allow for a downloadable algorithm/bot file to be downloaded so that it can be plugged into any instance of this app via a file upload, even if the instance of the app does not have the benefit of the trading/training data.\r\n\r\nThis means that the app should be configured so that each market has a configurable trader section that determines the type of trading algorithms (even a section to upload a custom python trading script for that market), and all the possible the configurable values for the algorithm, etc.\r\n\r\nAssess these in light of the future updates you just shared.\r\n\r\nYes, this is feasible. Your proposal would turn the app into a market-specific trading and research platform. The market selection and mini-graphs are straightforward; dependable learning, model portability, and custom Python uploads require a larger upgrade.\r\n\r\nThese are proposed changes\xe2\x80\x94the current v1.1 records trades but does not train or import algorithms.\r\n\r\nYour proposed market line-up\r\n\r\nI checked Deriv\xe2\x80\x99s public market catalog and available contract metadata. All eight markets were listed, with o'b'ne important execution difference:\r\n\r\nMarket\tSuggested abbreviated label\tFit with the current app\r\nEUR/USD\tEUR/USD\tRise/Fall listed; trade when open and the quote fits\r\nAUD/USD\tAUD/USD\tRise/Fall listed; same checks\r\nGBP/USD\tGBP/USD\tRise/Fall listed; same checks\r\nVolatility 100 (1s) Index\tV100\xe2\x80\x931s\tRise/Fall listed\r\nVolatility 75 (1s) Index\tV75\xe2\x80\x931s\tRise/Fall listed\r\nStep Index\tSTEP100\tThe catalog identifies the original instrument as Step Index 100; Rise/Fall listed\r\nCrash 500 Index\tCRASH500\tNeeds another execution adapter\r\nBoom 500 Index\tBOOM500\tNeeds another execution adapter\r\n\r\nCrash/Boom currently list Multipliers and Accumulators through the Options API, rather than Rise/Fall. Deriv also offers CFD routes through MT5/cTrader. We should therefore distinguish available to observe from supported for trading by this app. Final availability must be checked for the user\xe2\x80\x99s account, selected product, duration, and stake.\r\n\r\nI would use these eight as a curated line-up and retain your maximum of five selected markets.\r\n\r\nMarket suggestions should explain suitability\r\n\r\nThe user should remain responsible for selecting markets. The app can suggest markets based on current evidence, without automatically selecting or switching them.\r\n\r\nSuitable inputs include:\r\n\r\nDeriv\xe2\x80\x99s live feed: prices, data freshness, market availability, supported contracts, quoted payouts, and minimum trade sizes.\r\nThe app\xe2\x80\x99s own validated results: per'b'formance of a compatible strategy in that market, after costs.\r\nFor currency pairs: relevant economic announcements and official release calendars, with clearly identified sources.\r\n\r\nEach suggestion should show why it appeared, its source, when the data was updated, and any missing information. For example: \xe2\x80\x9cMarket open; compatible quote available; model passed recent validation.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nIf data is stale or evidence is insufficient, the result should be \xe2\x80\x9cNo recommendation available.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nFor the derived indices, external forex news should not be treated as a directional predictor. Deriv describes its synthetic prices as generated independently of external market data.\r\n\r\nThe selected-market mini-graphs are practical\r\n\r\nAfter selection, the app can display a horizontal row containing up to five compact market panels. Each would contain:\r\n\r\nAbbreviated name and live mini-graph.\r\nLatest price and feed status.\r\nTraining progress.\r\nOpen-position indicator.\r\n\r\nClicking either the name or graph would select that market in the larger Market observation section immediately below. The selected panel would be highlighted. On narrower screens, the row could scroll horizontally.\r\n\r\nChanging the displayed graph would not change which markets the bot is authorized to trade.\r\n\r\nPer-market learning needs more than a win/loss counter\r\n\r\nEach demo run can contribute training data for its selected markets. However, we should store the cir'b'cumstances surrounding decisions, not just whether completed trades won.\r\n\r\nRecord\tWhat it should capture\r\nMarket observation\tTimestamp, price data, calculated features, and source\r\nDecision\tEnter, wait, or reject\xe2\x80\x94with the reason\r\nProposed trade\tDirection, product, duration, quote, and expected return\r\nExecuted trade\tEntry, settlement, costs, outcome, and strategy version\r\nTraining run\tDataset period, model settings, results, and resulting version\r\n\r\nThis matters particularly for learning when not to enter. Executed trades alone cannot show whether rejected opportunities would have performed better or worse. We can record hypothetical outcomes for skipped opportunities separately; those must never appear as actual trades or account profit.\r\n\r\nThe existing local SQLite database is a useful foundation. Its current recent-200-trade statistic would need a separate cumulative eligible-trade counter for your 500-trade baseline.\r\n\r\nTraining data must also preserve the distinction between:\r\n\r\nFictional simulator outcomes.\r\nLive-price paper trades.\r\nDeriv virtual-account trades.\r\n\r\nFictional random-walk trades should not qualify a real-market model as trained. Replaying the same dataset repeatedly should not inflate the count of unique training examples.\r\n\r\nUse 500 trades as a minimum baseline, with validation tracked separately\r\n\r\nYour threshold is workable as a product rule. I would display these states:\r\n\r\nState\tMeaning\r\nDefau'b'lt algorithm\tNo eligible market-specific training history\r\nCollecting data: 240/500 \xe2\x80\x94 48%\tPartially complete baseline\r\nBaseline complete\tAt least 500 eligible trades; training or validation still pending\r\nTrained\xe2\x80\x94validation pending\tA fitted model exists but has not passed evaluation\r\nValidated model available\tThe model passed the defined evaluation checks\r\nReview required\tResults have deteriorated or compatibility has changed\r\n\r\nFive hundred trades do not establish that a strategy is profitable. Validation should examine net results, drawdown, consistency across periods, and performance on later data excluded from training. Features must contain only information available at the decision time; otherwise future information can leak into training and exaggerate performance.\r\n\r\nThe baseline should apply to a compatible profile: market + product + duration/timeframe + strategy version. Five hundred short-duration Rise/Fall trades should not automatically qualify a CFD strategy.\r\n\r\nThe default algorithm can operate under the existing risk controls while collecting evidence. It must remain free to wait; reaching 500 trades should never become a reason to force entries.\r\n\r\nDownloadable and uploadable algorithms are feasible\r\n\r\nA dedicated Market Algorithms section could show each market\xe2\x80\x99s profiles, training progress, validation status, active version, and export/import controls.\r\n\r\nAn exported bot package should include:\r\n\r'b'\nMarket and product compatibility.\r\nStrategy version and configurable parameters.\r\nFeature definitions, preprocessing, and learned model state.\r\nTraining counts, dates, provenance, and validation results.\r\nRequired app/model format versions.\r\n\r\nThat package can perform inference on another compatible installation without the original training database. However, independently reproducing its training results would require the underlying data.\r\n\r\nAn imported profile should distinguish its reported history from local history\xe2\x80\x94for example:\r\n\r\nImported model: 820 source training trades \xc2\xb7 0 local trades\r\n\r\nIt should not fabricate 820 local database records. Partially trained checkpoints can also be exported, retaining their incomplete status. Resuming training may require additional training state, depending on the algorithm.\r\n\r\nPer-market configuration should sit beneath the shared risk engine\r\n\r\nEach market\xe2\x80\x99s trader section can offer:\r\n\r\nConfiguration area\tExamples\r\nAlgorithm\tDefault rules, trained model, imported profile, custom script\r\nProduct\tSupported Rise/Fall or a future Multipliers/CFD adapter\r\nParameters\tIndicator periods, entry thresholds, confidence thresholds\r\nExecution\tContract duration, requested stake, cooldown, trading hours\r\nLearning\tTraining schedule, dataset window, candidate model settings\r\nLifecycle\tActive version, previous version, validation status, rollback\r\n\r\nGlobal allocation, floor, exposure, and 'b"cruise limits must remain authoritative. A market profile can request a trade; it cannot override those limits.\r\n\r\nCustom Python uploads are possible, but they introduce executable code into the PC. I would put that feature in an advanced section, with explicit activation, restricted operating-system isolation, resource limits, and no broker credentials. Scripts should return signals to the main engine rather than place orders themselves. A separate Python process alone is insufficient isolation.\r\n\r\nHow this changes the earlier update priorities\r\n\r\nStage\tRecommended scope\r\nNext: market workspace\tCurated line-up, five mini-graphs, click-through observation, source-backed suggestions, market profiles, richer database logging, and training progress\r\nThen: research and learning\tHistorical backtesting, strategy comparison, per-market training, chronological validation, versioning, and rollback\r\nThen: portability\tExport/import of trained profiles and partial checkpoints, compatibility checks, and imported-model status\r\nAdditional execution and extensions\tMultipliers support for Crash/Boom, isolated custom Python scripts, and external notifications\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is to build the market workspace and training-data foundation first. That gives subsequent learning and portable models the evidence and compatibility controls they need, while preserving your existing capital protection rules.\r\n\r\nLet's further explore these other "b'potential updates and the assess and prioritize. \r\n\r\nCan each market algorithm (whether partially or fully trained) indicate its success/win rate? Each rate starting at 0, and adjusting as more trades and training is done in the demo. When live trading is enabled through its Deriv integration, the data should still be stored and algorithms trained as well. \r\n\r\nI also want the option to create a new bot/algorithm for a market. The user can then name it and select it for training on all or a subset of the collected data specific to that market. The bot will adjust the algorithm and configurations necessary to be most successful at picking and winning trades in that market. \r\n\r\nAll algorithms will display their win rate.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94all of these updates are feasible. They fit together as a Bot Manager, where each market can have several named algorithms, each with its own configuration, training history, versions, and performance record.\r\n\r\nThe important distinction is between training an algorithm on historical data and measuring how it performs on new trades.\r\n\r\nEvery algorithm can display its win rate\r\n\r\nA new bot can initially show:\r\n\r\nWin rate: 0% \xc2\xb7 0 settled trades \xc2\xb7 No results yet\r\n\r\nThat satisfies your preferred starting display without suggesting that it has already lost trades.\r\n\r\nFor the primary trading win rate, I recommend:\r\n\r\nWinning trades \xc3\xb7 all settled trades \xc3\x97 100\r\n\r\nA win means positive net profit after tra'b'ding costs. Break-even trades remain in the denominator; open positions, rejected orders, and cancelled/refunded orders are shown separately.\r\n\r\nEach algorithm should display these results distinctly:\r\n\r\nMetric\tWhat it measures\r\nHistorical validation win rate\tPerformance on historical data excluded from training\r\nForward demo win rate\tNew demo trades made after the algorithm\xe2\x80\x99s version was established\r\nLive win rate\tSettled real-money trades, once live integration is implemented\r\nRecent win rate\tResults over a clearly stated recent window\r\nTraining progress\tEligible training records collected toward the 500-trade baseline\r\n\r\nThe main displayed rate should identify its source and sample size\xe2\x80\x94for example, \xe2\x80\x9cDemo: 61% \xc2\xb7 200 settled trades.\xe2\x80\x9d Training-set accuracy should not be presented as demonstrated trading success.\r\n\r\nAlso show net profit, drawdown, and average win versus average loss alongside win rate. For example, 90 wins earning $0.10 each and 10 losses costing $1 each produce a 90% win rate but a $1 loss.\r\n\r\nNamed bots and user-selected training data\r\n\r\nThe proposed creation process is practical:\r\n\r\nSelect a market.\r\nName the bot\xe2\x80\x94for example, EUR/USD Session Filter.\r\nChoose an algorithm template or compatible imported model.\r\nSelect its product, timeframe/duration, and configurable parameters.\r\nSelect all compatible market data or a subset.\r\nTrain and evaluate a candidate version.\r\nSelect it for forward demo 'b'trading.\r\n\r\nUseful dataset filters would include:\r\n\r\nFilter\tPurpose\r\nDate range\tTest recent conditions or a broader history\r\nTrading session\tExamine time-of-day differences\r\nProduct and duration\tKeep the training relevant to the intended trades\r\nData source\tDistinguish historical replay, paper demo, broker demo, and live\r\nMarket conditions\tStudy volatility, trend, or other recorded conditions\r\nData quality\tExclude incomplete, duplicate, or unreliable records\r\n\r\nWin-only or loss-only datasets should be available for diagnosis, but not presented as balanced training evidence. They omit part of the decision problem.\r\n\r\nA separate evaluation period should remain outside the training selection. Time-ordered evaluation is important because ordinary random splits can allow a model to learn from future observations while being evaluated on earlier ones.\r\n\r\nA newly created bot must earn its own performance record\r\n\r\nSuppose the PC contains 1,000 EUR/USD trades collected by the default algorithm. A new named bot may use compatible records from that dataset.\r\n\r\nIts display could then read:\r\n\r\nTraining records: 1,000 / 500 baseline met\r\nHistorical validation: 58% \xc2\xb7 180 evaluated trades\r\nForward demo: 0% \xc2\xb7 0 settled trades\r\n\r\nIt must not inherit the default bot\xe2\x80\x99s win rate. Its historical evaluation must replay its own decisions against the market data.\r\n\r\nThis makes storing price history, decision-time features, quotes, and skipped'b' opportunities essential. A table of previous wins and losses alone is insufficient for evaluating a different entry strategy.\r\n\r\nAutomatic adjustment is possible, within defined boundaries\r\n\r\nThe app can search for better-performing configurations, including:\r\n\r\nIndicator periods and entry thresholds.\r\nTrading sessions and market-condition filters.\r\nConfidence thresholds and rules for waiting.\r\nCompatible trade durations.\r\nModel parameters and feature combinations.\r\n\r\nHowever, \xe2\x80\x9cmost successful\xe2\x80\x9d should mean better measured performance after costs, subject to drawdown and risk constraints. Maximizing win rate alone could favour trades whose small winnings do not compensate for occasional losses.\r\n\r\nThe tuning process should compare candidates on validation data, then evaluate the selected candidate on a separate test period. Repeatedly adjusting parameters against the same test results can overfit that test set.\r\n\r\nThe optimizer should never change your allocation, protective floor, maximum exposure, or cruise limits. Those remain controlled by the central risk engine.\r\n\r\nLearning can continue during both demo and future live trading\r\n\r\nBoth modes can record completed trades and contribute eligible data to future training. I recommend this process:\r\n\r\nRecord the decision \xe2\x86\x92 settle and score the trade \xe2\x86\x92 add the completed observation \xe2\x86\x92 train a candidate \xe2\x86\x92 validate it \xe2\x86\x92 make a new version available.\r\n\r\nThe active 'b'trading version should remain identifiable and stable while its replacement is being evaluated. Updating a model should not alter an already-open trade\xe2\x80\x99s management.\r\n\r\nLive and demo outcomes should retain separate labels and performance figures. Combining their training data can be an explicit option, because execution and settlement differences can affect results.\r\n\r\nFor every version, preserve:\r\n\r\nWhich data trained it.\r\nWhich data evaluated it.\r\nIts configuration and creation date.\r\nTrades actually attributed to that version.\r\nThe previous version for rollback.\r\n\r\nYour 500-trade requirement remains a minimum training-data baseline, not an automatic certificate of readiness. Retraining the same records repeatedly does not increase the number of unique training records.\r\n\r\nHow I would prioritize the expanded requirements\r\n\r\nPriority\tUpdate\tWhy it comes here\r\n1 \xe2\x80\x94 Foundation\tNamed bot registry, version identifiers, richer market/decision database, accurate per-bot metrics\tEverything else depends on correct attribution and sufficient data\r\n2 \xe2\x80\x94 User controls\tMarket mini-graphs, training progress, per-market bot selection and configuration\tMakes the system understandable and manageable\r\n3 \xe2\x80\x94 Research\tDataset selection, historical replay, chronological validation, strategy comparison\tEstablishes how candidates will be judged\r\n4 \xe2\x80\x94 Learning\tPer-market training, constrained parameter tuning, candidate comparison and roll'b'back\tAdds adaptation to an established evaluation process\r\n5 \xe2\x80\x94 Portability\tExport/import models, configurations, provenance, and reported performance\tAllows reuse without fabricating local trading history\r\n6 \xe2\x80\x94 Advanced execution\tAdditional product adapters, isolated custom Python scripts, then separately validated live trading\tRequires the earlier controls to be dependable\r\n\r\nSeveral named bots can research or run in shadow mode for the same market. Initially, I recommend one executing bot per selected market, with all five markets sharing your existing capital controls. This lets you compare algorithms without accidentally multiplying account exposure.\r\n\r\nThe next substantive upgrade should therefore combine the bot registry, per-bot performance tracking, richer data collection, and the market workspace. Those provide the foundation for meaningful training rather than simply a rising trade counter.\r\n\r\nLet\'s further expand on the future updates potential:\r\n\r\nHow about we establish a standard for portable "setup" files. Users will be able to buy and use as is, or configure these setup files for each market. They may also sell these files back to other users with their boasted success rates. These files are uploadable into the app as a proprietary file type.\r\n\r\nWe should have several categories of setup files:\r\n\r\nbot - for full setup, including bot, algorithm and all variable configurations set.\r\nsetup - configuration da'b"ta for bots\r\ntraining - training to update a bot's configuration based on successful training on the data\r\n\r\nThese files should have a standard format, that is also easy for a general user to fill/modify. Training data should be csv format.\r\n\r\nYes. A standard package format would make bots easier to configure, transfer, update, and eventually sell. I would establish the format before building a marketplace, so every installation interprets the files consistently.\r\n\r\nThe key distinction is between a complete bot, its settings, a trained update, and the raw data used for training.\r\n\r\nProposed file categories\r\n\r\nThe extensions below are working names:\r\n\r\nCategory\tProposed extension\tContents\tImport behaviour\r\nBot\t.tbot\tAlgorithm, learned model where applicable, configuration, market compatibility, documentation, and performance evidence\tCreates a new bot or installs a specific version\r\nSetup\t.tsetup\tConfiguration values for an identified compatible algorithm\tPreviews changes to an existing bot; does not replace its algorithm\r\nTraining update\t.ttrain\tLearned model/state and configuration changes produced by training, plus evaluation results and provenance\tCreates a candidate updated version of a compatible bot\r\nTraining data\t.csv\tMarket observations, decisions, trades, outcomes, or other supported training records\tAdds eligible records to the local database; training is a separate action\r\n\r\nA CSV dataset and a trained update ar"b'e different deliverables. Users could import CSV data to train their own bots, or import a .ttrain package containing the result of someone else\xe2\x80\x99s training.\r\n\r\nA training update should identify the algorithm and version it requires. It must not silently apply to an incompatible bot.\r\n\r\nOne shared format underneath the three package types\r\n\r\nI recommend a ZIP-based container with the proprietary extension, containing:\r\n\r\nComponent\tPurpose\r\nManifest\tPackage type, standard version, identity, author, and compatibility\r\nConfiguration\tReadable settings and their values\r\nAlgorithm/model\tRequired implementation or learned state\r\nPerformance report\tResults, evaluation conditions, and supporting evidence\r\nDocumentation\tDescription and instructions\r\nLicence\tEditing, sharing, commercial-use, and resale permissions\r\nIntegrity information\tDetects changes and, where signed, identifies the publisher\r\n\r\nThis gives users a recognizable application-specific file while keeping the underlying structure portable.\r\n\r\nA proprietary extension does not itself provide encryption, copy protection, or exclusive ownership. Those would be separate commercial decisions.\r\n\r\nMake configuration easy through the app\r\n\r\nGeneral users should mainly work through an editor, rather than manually editing package internals.\r\n\r\nThe editor could provide:\r\n\r\nA plain-language description beside each setting.\r\nSupported values, defaults, and validation.\r\nBasic and adv'b'anced views.\r\nA preview of changes before applying them.\r\nSave setup, Export bot, and Restore previous version actions.\r\n\r\nFor users who prefer manual editing, the configuration can remain readable JSON. For example:\r\n\r\n{\r\n  "format_version": "1.0",\r\n  "package_type": "setup",\r\n  "name": "EUR/USD Session Setup",\r\n  "market": "frxEURUSD",\r\n  "requires_algorithm": {\r\n    "id": "session-momentum",\r\n    "version": "1.2"\r\n  },\r\n  "settings": {\r\n    "duration_seconds": 300,\r\n    "fast_period": 10,\r\n    "slow_period": 40,\r\n    "cooldown_seconds": 120\r\n  }\r\n}\r\n\r\nThe application would validate this against its registered algorithm definitions. A seller cannot make an invalid setting acceptable merely by declaring it valid in the file.\r\n\r\nPerformance belongs to the exact configuration that produced it\r\n\r\nThis is particularly important if users will sell setups with advertised success rates.\r\n\r\nA reported rate should identify:\r\n\r\nRequired context\tExample\r\nMarket and product\tEUR/USD, Rise/Fall\r\nAlgorithm and configuration version\tVersion 1.2, configuration fingerprint\r\nEvidence source\tHistorical validation, forward demo, or live\r\nEvaluation period\tStart and end dates\r\nSample size\t620 settled trades\r\nOutcome counts\tWins, losses, and break-even trades\r\nFinancial results\tNet trading profit, average win/loss, and drawdown\r\nAssumptions\tPayouts, costs, execution method, and exclusions\r\n\r\nChanging performance-relevant settings creates a new 'b'configuration version. The original results remain visible as historical evidence, but they do not become the new configuration\xe2\x80\x99s own results.\r\n\r\nFor example:\r\n\r\nPurchased setup: Seller reports 68% across 620 demo trades.\r\nBuyer\xe2\x80\x99s installation: Local demo 0% across 0 settled trades.\r\nBuyer modifies its entry threshold: New configuration\xe2\x80\x94no evaluated results yet.\r\n\r\nThis prevents users from modifying a successful setup and continuing to advertise the original rate as though the modified version achieved it.\r\n\r\nSeparate claims from verification\r\n\r\nI would use explicit evidence labels:\r\n\r\nLabel\tWhat it establishes\r\nSeller-reported\tThe publisher supplied the figures\r\nRecomputed from supplied records\tThe app reproduced the arithmetic from the included records\r\nReconciled with broker records\tApplicable trade records were matched against broker information\r\nLocally evaluated\tThis installation generated the displayed results\r\n\r\nRecomputing a CSV does not prove that its trades occurred or that the dataset includes every loss. A signature establishes publisher identity and file integrity; it does not establish profitability.\r\n\r\nTherefore, editable descriptions may contain a seller\xe2\x80\x99s claim, but the app\xe2\x80\x99s calculated performance fields should be generated from identified evidence. No package should receive a blanket \xe2\x80\x9cverified winning bot\xe2\x80\x9d badge.\r\n\r\nTraining CSV standards should support different record types\r\n\r\nOne enormo'b'us CSV template would be difficult for general users to manage. I recommend separate templates sharing common identifiers.\r\n\r\nCSV template\tPrincipal fields\r\nPrices\tMarket, UTC timestamp, price or OHLC values, timeframe, source\r\nTrades\tTrade ID, bot/version, market, product, direction, entry/exit times, stake, costs, net profit, mode\r\nDecisions\tDecision ID, market, timestamp, algorithm version, enter/wait/reject, reason, feature values\r\n\r\nSome products require additional fields: an option needs its duration and quoted payout; a CFD needs position size, entry/exit prices, and relevant charges.\r\n\r\nThe importer should validate dates, units, duplicates, missing values, market identity, and product compatibility. It should show accepted and rejected row counts before committing the import.\r\n\r\nYour 500-trade baseline should count eligible unique records, not repeated imports of the same trades. Imported market data also must not alter account balances or appear as newly executed trades.\r\n\r\nPackages must not override the account\xe2\x80\x99s controls\r\n\r\nA purchased bot can arrive fully configured and ready for demo evaluation. Importing it should still require reviewing its market, configuration, and permissions.\r\n\r\nThe following should remain outside portable packages:\r\n\r\nAPI tokens and account credentials.\r\nAccount balances and personal information.\r\nAuthorization to use live money.\r\nPermission to weaken the global floor or exposure limi'b'ts.\r\n\r\nA bot can propose orders; the app\xe2\x80\x99s central risk engine decides whether they are permitted.\r\n\r\nExecutable algorithms also need a separate trust boundary. Python\xe2\x80\x99s own documentation warns that formats such as pickle can execute arbitrary code, and CPython does not provide an untrusted-code sandbox. A renamed file extension does not address that risk.\r\n\r\nFor the first package standard, I recommend supporting registered built-in algorithms and controlled model formats. Arbitrary Python plugins should follow later with operating-system isolation.\r\n\r\nSelling and reselling need explicit permissions\r\n\r\nBeing able to edit a purchased setup should not automatically grant permission to resell it.\r\n\r\nEach package should state whether it permits:\r\n\r\nPersonal use.\r\nModification.\r\nRedistribution.\r\nResale of the original.\r\nCommercial distribution of derivatives.\r\n\r\nModified packages should retain their parent package\xe2\x80\x99s identity and author attribution. Their performance records should identify the modified version separately.\r\n\r\nLicensing information can travel with an offline file, but enforcement against copying is a separate problem. A future marketplace can add publisher identities, purchase entitlements, listings, and dispute handling.\r\n\r\nRevised priorities\r\n\r\nPriority\tDeliverable\r\n1\tStandard identifiers, bot/configuration versions, database attribution, and performance definitions\r\n2\tReadable configuration schema, edito'b'r, .tsetup import/export, and CSV templates\r\n3\tPer-market training, validation, and compatible .ttrain updates\r\n4\tComplete .tbot packages, provenance, signatures, and permission controls\r\n5\tMarketplace, publisher reputation, purchases, and resale workflows\r\n6\tIsolated custom Python plugins and stronger commercial protection\r\n\r\nI would make Trading Package Standard 1.0 the next design specification. It should define the three package categories together, while implementing setup files and CSV imports first. That gives future training, full bot exports, and sales a consistent foundation.\r\n\r\nShow the prioritized list of updates and their corresponding app version proposed\r\n\r\nThe current release is v1.1. I propose the following roadmap, with reliable data and evaluation established before automated learning or live trading.\r\n\r\nPriority\tProposed version\tMain updates\r\n1\tv1.2 \xe2\x80\x94 Market Workspace\tCurated eight-market line-up; select up to five; live mini-graphs with abbreviated names; click to expand in Market Observation; source-backed market suggestions with timestamps and availability checks. Unsupported products remain observation-only.\r\n2\tv1.3 \xe2\x80\x94 Bot Manager & Performance\tCreate and name market-specific bots; select one executing bot per market; per-bot configuration and version history; separate historical, demo, and live win rates; sample counts, net profit, drawdown, and 500-trade baseline progress.\r\n3\tv1.4 \xe2\x80\x94 Data & Se'b'tup Standard\tRicher local database for observations, decisions, trades, and provenance; validated CSV imports/exports; dataset selection; Trading Package Standard 1.0; configuration editor and .tsetup import/export.\r\n4\tv1.5 \xe2\x80\x94 Backtesting & Validation\tHistorical replay; strategy comparison; chronological training/validation/test separation; cost-aware evaluation; explicit readiness criteria and reproducible performance reports.\r\n5\tv1.6 \xe2\x80\x94 Market-Specific Learning\tTrain named bots on compatible market datasets; constrained configuration optimization; default algorithms until trained; candidate-versus-active model comparison; deterioration detection and rollback.\r\n6\tv1.7 \xe2\x80\x94 Portable Bots & Training\tComplete .tbot packages and .ttrain updates; compatibility checks; imported-versus-local performance labels; provenance, integrity checks, and licence permissions.\r\n7\tv1.8 \xe2\x80\x94 Expanded Execution & Protection\tMultipliers adapter for eligible markets, including Crash/Boom; product-specific sizing and recovery; configurable daily loss, losing-streak, and profit-protection controls; combined exposure testing.\r\n8\tv1.9 \xe2\x80\x94 Remote Management\tExternal approval requests and alerts; connection monitoring; backup/restore; operating-health dashboard; optional hosted deployment.\r\n9\tv2.0 \xe2\x80\x94 Controlled Live Trading\tExplicit live-account activation; broker reconciliation and recovery validation; separate live metrics; live-data collection and'b" candidate training; deployment approval and rollback controls.\r\n10\tv2.1 \xe2\x80\x94 Advanced Strategy Plugins\tIsolated custom Python algorithms; approved dependencies; resource limits; controlled access to market data; signals routed through the central risk engine.\r\n11\tv2.2 \xe2\x80\x94 Marketplace\tPublisher profiles; package purchases and permitted resale; version-specific performance disclosures; evidence labels; entitlements, ratings, and dispute workflows.\r\n\r\nRelease gates matter more than version numbers: 500 eligible trades establishes the training baseline, validation determines model readiness, and live trading requires its own execution checks. Marketplace performance claims should depend on the evidence standards introduced earlier.\r\n\r\nLet's discuss these additional features before including in the list of prioritized updates:\r\nInclude a feature that allows the user to connect their Deric account to switch to live trading in real markets, with sufficient warnings and verifications before switching.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94assuming you mean Deriv. This would expand the proposed v2.0 Controlled Live Trading feature, rather than create a separate roadmap item. I would call it Deriv Account Connection & Live Activation.\r\n\r\nThe app should distinguish three modes clearly:\r\n\r\nMode\tPrices\tMoney used\r\nLive-price paper demo\tCurrent market data\tFictional local funds\r\nDeriv account demo\tBroker market data\tDeriv virtual funds\r\nLive trading\tBroker market d"b'ata\tReal account funds\r\n\r\nConnecting an account must not automatically authorize trading. Deriv supports OAuth authorization without sharing the user\xe2\x80\x99s password, as well as Personal Access Tokens. I would prefer a Connect with Deriv authorization flow for the finished app, subject to the application\xe2\x80\x99s registration and integration requirements.\r\n\r\nProposed activation process\r\n\r\nConnect and identify the account. Display the selected account, currency, balance, and whether it is real or virtual. Initially permit account inspection only.\r\nPause demo entries. For the initial implementation, require existing demo positions to settle before switching modes. Retain their history separately.\r\nSelect the live markets and bots. Check account-specific product availability, minimum stakes, durations, permissions, and current quotes.\r\nApprove live capital and cycle length. Show the opening floor, permitted loss allowance, stake limits, and aggregate exposure across all selected markets.\r\nReview warnings and verification results.\r\nExplicitly activate live trading. Use a clearly labelled action such as \xe2\x80\x9cEnable real-money trading\xe2\x80\x9d, followed by the normal Start entries control.\r\n\r\nDeriv provides separate public, demo, and real trading endpoints, so the application can enforce this distinction in its connection and execution logic.\r\n\r\nWarnings should be specific to the proposed allocation\r\n\r\nFor a $50 allocation, the confirmation sho'b'uld explain:\r\n\r\nReal money is at risk.\r\nThe opening protective floor is $40, representing a $10 planned loss allowance.\r\nThe floor rises under your agreed rules; it is not insurance against execution, broker, or software failures.\r\nDemo and historical results do not guarantee live results.\r\nWhich markets, bot versions, and settings will receive permission to trade.\r\n\r\nThe warning should appear on first activation and when material exposure changes\xe2\x80\x94not before every ordinary trade.\r\n\r\nVerification should produce clear outcomes\r\n\r\nCheck\tProposed outcome if unsuccessful\r\nAccount identity or real/demo status cannot be established\tBlock activation\r\nInsufficient funds or missing trading permission\tBlock activation\r\nUnsupported market, product, duration, or stake\tDisable the affected selection\r\nUnresolved orders or broker/local-ledger mismatch\tBlock new entries until reconciled\r\nSelected bot has not passed the agreed validation requirements\tBlock automatic live execution\r\nMarket data is stale or the connection is unhealthy\tBlock new entries\r\n\r\nYour 500-trade baseline should remain part of bot readiness, alongside validation. A high advertised win rate or imported \xe2\x80\x9ctrained\xe2\x80\x9d label alone should not satisfy live activation.\r\n\r\nSeveral switching rules need to be explicit\r\n\r\nDemo approvals do not carry over into live mode. Real capital requires separate authorization.\r\nThe $30 fallback must not activate real trading for the first 'b"time. During live onboarding, the user can separately enable that fallback for later cycle renewals. Otherwise, an unanswered live renewal pauses entries.\r\nSwitching back to demo does not close real positions. Pause live entries, show outstanding positions, and continue their defined management until settled or deliberately closed where supported.\r\nDisconnecting or closing the app does not remove broker exposure. Show outstanding positions before disconnecting.\r\nRestarting restores live position monitoring, with new entries paused. It should not silently switch an existing real account into a simulated ledger.\r\n\r\nA persistent REAL MONEY indicator, selected account identifier, and visible pause control should remain on screen whenever live execution is enabled.\r\n\r\nLive learning can continue without changing the active bot unexpectedly\r\n\r\nLive trades would be recorded in the native database and attributed to their exact bot version. They could train candidate updates, while the executing version remains stable. Replacing it would require the agreed validation and deployment process; training must never loosen the central risk controls.\r\n\r\nI would retain this under proposed v2.0, with account connection, activation permissions, broker reconciliation, and recovery treated as release requirements. The current v1.1 remains demo-only; this discussion does not enable real-money trading.\r\n\r\nI'll implement all these versions, one at"b' a time for testing over the next 2-3 days, starting today. Keep a record of completed implementations and my feedback on each until all are complete. If my feedback creates a need for further updates to be added, confirm with me the inclusion of the new feature, requirement or update, and then add it to list to track. Provide daily reminders until complete. Once complete, provide a report and recommendations based on all the implementations, my comments, etc.\r\n\r\nDone. I\xe2\x80\x99ve created the \r\nimplementation and feedback tracker and scheduled daily evening reminders around 7 PM, starting today, until completion.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99ll record each version\xe2\x80\x99s implementation, test results, your feedback, outstanding issues, and acceptance. Any new feature or requirement arising from feedback will need your confirmation before joining the approved backlog.\r\n\r\nOnce completion is confirmed, I\xe2\x80\x99ll produce the consolidated report and recommendations, then stop the reminders.\r\n\r\nStarting position: v1.1 is delivered, your testing feedback is pending, and v1.2 is next. Share results here as you test\xe2\x80\x94I cannot automatically observe your PC.\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nThe web-app should allow the user to setup an account using their verified email and password, or via their Gmail or Github accounts. If possible, two-factor should be enabled. If this authentication/security feature can be toggled on/off in the developer version for'b' testing then that would be great as well.\r\n\r\nThe user should also be able to personalize their personal account in an active session once logged in. Information, such as display image, name, username, etc.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94these are feasible and should be introduced before Bot Manager, because bots, trading histories, and Deriv connections will need clear account ownership.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded this as CR-001: Accounts, Security & Profiles, pending your confirmation.\r\n\r\nFeature\tProposed behaviour\r\nEmail/password registration\tVerify email through an expiring link; support secure password storage and password recovery.\r\nGoogle/Gmail sign-in\tSign in through Google without granting access to the user\xe2\x80\x99s inbox.\r\nGitHub sign-in\tAuthorize through GitHub without requesting repository access.\r\nTwo-factor authentication\tAuthenticator-app codes with single-use recovery codes.\r\nProfile personalization\tEdit profile image, name/display name, and unique username while logged in; changes persist immediately.\r\nAccount management\tChange password, reverify a changed email, manage connected sign-in methods, and sign out.\r\nData separation\tKeep each user\xe2\x80\x99s bots, trading/training records, settings, and Deriv credentials separate.\r\n\r\nGoogle provides email-verification claims, while GitHub exposes verified-email information through its email API. We should check that information rather than assume every social-login account has supplied a usable verifie'b'd email.\r\n\r\nFor two-factor authentication, I recommend making it optional during paper-demo testing and required before real-money activation. Changing or recovering security settings should require appropriate identity checks. Email verification and two-factor authentication remain separate steps.\r\n\r\nThe developer toggle is also feasible, with these boundaries:\r\n\r\nControlled through developer startup configuration.\r\nRestricted to local development and a separate test identity/database.\r\nClearly labelled \xe2\x80\x9cDevelopment mode\xe2\x80\x94authentication bypassed.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\nReal-money trading disabled while bypassed.\r\nProduction refuses to start with authentication bypass enabled.\r\n\r\nTurning the bypass off would let you test the complete registration, sign-in, verification, and two-factor flows.\r\n\r\nProfile edits should preserve a stable internal account identity: changing a username must not change ownership of bots, purchases, or trading records. Linking Google or GitHub to an existing account should require authentication to that account.\r\n\r\nConfirm adding \xe2\x80\x9cAccounts, Security & Profiles\xe2\x80\x9d as a dedicated release before Bot Manager, including the developer-only authentication toggle? This confirmation follows your instruction to approve new requirements before they enter the tracked roadmap.\r\n\r\nWhen a user starts a trading session...\r\n\r\nIs the trading data saved?\r\nIf yes, where is the trading data saved for later retrieval?\r\n\r\nYes. In the 'b'current v1.1, trading records are saved automatically on the PC running the Python app. They remain available after closing and restarting it.\r\n\r\nBy default, they are stored in the data folder beside app.py:\r\n\r\nTrading mode\tDatabase file\r\nOffline simulator\tdata/sandbox.sqlite3\r\nLive-price paper demo\tdata/market-demo.sqlite3\r\nDeriv virtual-account demo\tdata/deriv-demo.sqlite3\r\n\r\nThese are SQLite databases, with separate records for each mode. A custom --data launch argument changes the storage location.\r\n\r\nThe app currently saves:\r\n\r\nSettled trades: market, strategy, direction, stake, net profit/loss, timestamps, and cycle identifier.\r\nOpen positions and pending orders: information needed for restart and reconciliation.\r\nCycle state: allocation, balance, protective floor, duration, and cruise status.\r\nSettings and activity events.\r\n\r\nFor retrieval, use the Trade Journal in the app or Export all trades \xe2\x86\x92 CSV. The journal displays the latest 100 trades, but the database retains older records and the CSV export includes all settled trades.\r\n\r\nThe important limitation: v1.1 does not preserve a complete historical training dataset. It retains a rolling snapshot of roughly 200 price observations per market, rather than continuously archiving every tick, feature, quote, and skipped decision. That fuller recording belongs to the proposed Data & Setup upgrade.\r\n\r\nTo preserve existing history when upgrading, keep the data folder. F'b"or a complete backup, stop the Python app and copy that entire folder. Account-based separation and automatic cloud synchronization are not implemented yet.\r\n\r\nGeneral comment based on the overall project. Let's discuss:I want the user to be able to download the trading data for their own account. They specify the date period since their first session to the most recent/active session. They have the option to download it as csv or xlsx.\r\n\r\nWhen a user creates their own bot, it saves as a file on their local machine in downloads. After each trading session or training session, the user is prompted to save the changes to their bot. If not, the changes are lost. Only the admin user can create ad save public bots that are available to all users of the system. These public bots are stored on the server. All user-specific bots, and data are private and stored as local files in the user's downloads. Only the market specific configs are saved for each user on the server. As the configs are made by the user and saved on the site, the data is updated on the database for custom/dynamic user experience. Otherwise, the user upload their bot files, training files or configuration files when needed. The user should be able to download the current configuration or training data for their select markets. They will have the option to download the full bot, the config file only or the trading data only for their live session. The data is wri"b"tten to the user's temporary storage locally if not explicitly downloaded.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94this is feasible as a local-first design. Personal bots and trading datasets would stay on the user\xe2\x80\x99s device, while the server manages public bots and saved account settings.\r\n\r\nHowever, two requirements need reconciling: exporting history from the first session and discarding data that was never explicitly downloaded.\r\n\r\nProposed storage arrangement\r\n\r\nInformation\tProposed location\r\nAdmin-published public bots\tServer\r\nUser\xe2\x80\x99s saved market configurations\tServer, associated with their account\r\nAccount/profile/security records\tServer, if the earlier authentication proposal is approved\r\nPersonal bot files and learned models\tUser-selected local files\r\nPrivate trading and training datasets\tLocal device; downloadable as CSV/XLSX\r\nUnsaved bot changes\tLocal working storage\r\nLive-trading recovery information\tAutomatically saved locally\xe2\x80\x94recommended exception discussed below\r\n\r\nPrivate file imports should be processed on the device or by the local Python companion. Sending them to a hosted training backend would change this privacy model, even if the server deleted them afterward.\r\n\r\nDownloads and file saving\r\n\r\nThe app can offer these actions for selected markets:\r\n\r\nAction\tOutput\r\nDownload full bot\t.tbot: algorithm/model, configuration, version and provenance\r\nDownload configuration\t.tsetup: compatible configuration values\r\nDownload training up"b'date\t.ttrain: learned changes and evaluation metadata\r\nDownload trading data\tCSV or XLSX for the chosen period\r\nDownload training dataset\tCSV; optionally XLSX for inspection\r\n\r\nA browser cannot silently choose and write arbitrary files in Downloads. Normal downloads follow the browser\xe2\x80\x99s download settings. Supported browsers can also let the user explicitly select a file or folder and grant access for subsequent saves. We should support both approaches.\r\n\r\nTherefore, I would describe the destination as \xe2\x80\x9cDownloads or a location chosen by the user.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nSaving or discarding bot changes\r\n\r\nAt the end of a trading or training session, present:\r\n\r\nSave new bot version \xe2\x80\x94 preserve the updated model and configuration.\r\nDiscard bot changes \xe2\x80\x94 keep the previously saved version.\r\nReturn to session \xe2\x80\x94 postpone the decision.\r\n\r\nThese choices should apply to algorithm/model changes. Exporting the session\xe2\x80\x99s trading data should remain a separate option.\r\n\r\nAlso distinguish Save configuration to my account from Save bot file. If the user explicitly saves settings to the server, subsequently discarding a bot draft should not imply those server settings were undone.\r\n\r\nAn imported bot whose settings differ from saved account settings should trigger a comparison rather than silently merging them.\r\n\r\nWe cannot guarantee a save prompt when the browser crashes or is forcibly closed. The reliable approach is an explicit End Session acti'b'on, supplemented by an unsaved-changes warning where supported.\r\n\r\nDate-range exports need retained history\r\n\r\nThe export screen can provide:\r\n\r\nEarliest available record through the current session.\r\nCustom start/end dates.\r\nAccount, market, bot/version, and demo/live filters.\r\nCSV or XLSX.\r\nAn export timestamp, with open trades clearly distinguished from settled trades.\r\n\r\nBut if yesterday\xe2\x80\x99s undownloaded data was discarded, the app cannot export it today. Signing in on another device would retrieve server settings\xe2\x80\x94not private files that were never uploaded.\r\n\r\nThe app must therefore identify missing periods and allow users to import earlier datasets. It should never label an incomplete export \xe2\x80\x9ccomplete account history.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nTemporary local storage needs an explicit policy\r\n\r\nBrowser-managed storage can hold working data, but it is separate from Downloads. It is subject to storage limits and can be deleted when site data is cleared; it does not automatically become temporary merely because the app calls it temporary.\r\n\r\nWe should define when drafts are deleted, whether interrupted sessions can be recovered, and how different signed-in users are separated on a shared device. Local files are private from the service, but are not automatically encrypted or inaccessible to other computer users.\r\n\r\nLive-trading recovery should survive \xe2\x80\x9cDiscard changes\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nFor eventual real-money trading, I recommend a small, automat'b'ically saved local recovery record containing the cycle authorization, protective floor, and pending/open order references.\r\n\r\nDiscarding training changes must not:\r\n\r\nReset the protective floor.\r\nForget an outstanding order.\r\nStart a new cycle with fresh loss capacity.\r\nTreat real positions as though they disappeared.\r\n\r\nIf recovery information is unavailable, the app should block new entries until exposure and authorization can be reconciled.\r\n\r\nPublic bots and the marketplace\r\n\r\nAdmin-only publication is straightforward: only an authorized administrator can create or update the server\xe2\x80\x99s public bot catalogue.\r\n\r\nA user editing a public bot would create a private local copy. This changes the earlier marketplace proposal: users could export files for permitted private distribution, but could not directly publish server-hosted public bots. Resale permissions would still depend on the package licence.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded this discussion as CR-002, pending clarification and approval. It affects the data foundation, portable files, hosting, live recovery, and marketplace design.\r\n\r\nWhich history policy do you prefer before we finalize it?\r\n\r\nA \xe2\x80\x94 Retain private local history automatically: supports exports back to the first retained session; saving changes to the bot file still requires confirmation.\r\nB \xe2\x80\x94 Strictly discard undownloaded session data: exports cover only the current data and earlier files the user imports; com'b"plete historical retrieval cannot be guaranteed.\r\n\r\nI recommend A, with private local history kept separate from explicitly saved bot versions.\r\n\r\nA\r\n\r\nConfirmed\xe2\x80\x94Option A is recorded. The planned behaviour is:\r\n\r\nAutomatically retain private trading/training history locally.\r\nExport retained history by date range as CSV or XLSX.\r\nRequire explicit saving of bot/model changes.\r\nDiscarding bot changes preserves trading history.\r\n\r\nThe \r\ntracker is updated; this change has not yet been implemented.\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nThese are my additional ideas for the store. Let's discuss before approving:\r\nUsers can sell their bots directly to the store. If bought, customers can give the purchase a nick-name and personal description for their purposes. They will then be able to post their success rates with the push of a button directly from their session to the item in the store, with comments. Other users will be able to use it to justify the posted seller success rate. Also, customers can choose a purchased compatible bot for the specific market from a list of drop-downs when uploading a trading bot to use.\r\n\r\nThese ideas are feasible and fit an admin-controlled store. I\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded them as CR-003 for discussion only, without adding them to the approved roadmap.\r\n\r\nThe main distinction is between purchasing a bot, personalizing its name, modifying its behaviour, and reporting its results.\r\n\r\n1. Users sell "b'bots to the store\r\n\r\nI interpret this as the store acquiring a bot or resale licence, then selling it to customers:\r\n\r\nCreator submits a bot and supporting performance information.\r\nStore reviews compatibility, functionality, ownership/licensing, and evidence.\r\nStore accepts or declines the proposed purchase.\r\nAn administrator publishes an accepted version.\r\nCustomers purchase access to that version.\r\n\r\nThis preserves your earlier rule that only administrators publish public bots.\r\n\r\nThe acquisition terms need defining: buying a file, purchasing resale rights, and acquiring exclusive ownership are different arrangements.\r\n\r\n2. Customers personalize purchased bots\r\n\r\nA buyer could assign:\r\n\r\nA private nickname.\r\nA personal description.\r\nOptional private notes.\r\n\r\nThese should leave the public item\xe2\x80\x99s identity, author, and version unchanged.\r\n\r\nPublic store item\tBuyer\xe2\x80\x99s private label\r\nEUR/USD Session Trader \xe2\x80\x94 v1.2\t\xe2\x80\x9cMy morning EUR bot\xe2\x80\x9d\r\nSeller\xe2\x80\x99s description\t\xe2\x80\x9cUse during my selected morning session\xe2\x80\x9d\r\n\r\nRenaming a purchase would not change its performance identity. Changing its algorithm or performance-relevant configuration would create a customized version with separately attributed results.\r\n\r\nFor your local-first model, I recommend keeping these private labels locally initially. Synchronizing them across devices would require another permitted server-side preference.\r\n\r\n3. Publish session results to the store 'b'item\r\n\r\nA Post Results button is practical. It should calculate results automatically and open a preview before publication.\r\n\r\nA performance post should contain:\r\n\r\nInformation\tWhy it matters\r\nBot and configuration version\tIdentifies what actually ran\r\nMarket, product, and duration\tMakes comparisons meaningful\r\nHistorical, paper-demo, broker-demo, or live\tDistinguishes the evidence\r\nSession dates and reporting timestamp\tDefines the period\r\nSettled trades and wins/losses/break-even\tGives the percentage context\r\nNet trading profit/loss and measured drawdown\tShows more than win rate\r\nOriginal or customized setup\tSeparates results from modified versions\r\nCustomer comments\tExplains their experience\r\n\r\nIf posted during an active session, it should say \xe2\x80\x9cSession in progress\xe2\x80\x94as of [time]\xe2\x80\x9d. Open positions must not count as settled wins.\r\n\r\nRepeatedly pressing Post Results should update the same report or create a linked revision\xe2\x80\x94not count the same trades again.\r\n\r\n4. Customer reports can help assess seller claims\xe2\x80\x94but cannot prove them\r\n\r\nThe store could show three separate views:\r\n\r\nSeller-reported performance.\r\nCommunity-submitted performance.\r\nThe buyer\xe2\x80\x99s own local performance.\r\n\r\nFor example, buyers could see whether customers using the same version and original configuration report broadly similar results.\r\n\r\nHowever, these badges must remain distinct:\r\n\r\nBadge\tWhat it establishes\r\nVerified purchaser\tThe account pur'b'chased the item\r\nApp-reported results\tThe application supplied calculated figures\r\nBroker-reconciled outcomes\tSubmitted execution/outcome records were matched against broker information\r\n\r\nA verified purchase does not verify performance. Locally editable files can be manipulated, and even broker-confirmed trades do not independently prove which algorithm generated them.\r\n\r\nCommunity results also represent people who chose to publish, not necessarily every customer. Aggregates should identify participant and trade counts, separate comparable versions/modes, and include losing results. Platform guidance likewise emphasizes collecting and moderating reviews without improperly favouring positive feedback.\r\n\r\n5. Select purchased bots from compatible drop-downs\r\n\r\nThe market\xe2\x80\x99s trader panel could offer:\r\n\r\nSelection\tAvailable choices\r\nBot source\tDefault bot / My purchased bots / Import local bot\r\nBot\tCompatible purchases for that market\r\nVersion\tAvailable compatible versions\r\nConfiguration\tOriginal setup / saved account settings / imported setup\r\n\r\nThe app should filter by market, product, runtime compatibility, and purchase entitlement. Unsupported items could remain visible with an explanation.\r\n\r\nSelecting a purchased bot can load the original store package. If the user wants their privately trained or customized copy, they must provide that local file. The store cannot recover private changes that were never uploaded.\r\n\r\nTh'b'is introduces additional server records\r\n\r\nYour earlier storage proposal allowed server-hosted public bots and account configurations. These store features would also require:\r\n\r\nPurchase and licence entitlements.\r\nCreator submission/acquisition records.\r\nExplicitly published performance reports and comments.\r\n\r\nThat is a deliberate exception to the local-first policy. Posting results should not silently upload private bot models, complete trading history, credentials, or account balances. Any supporting evidence submission would need a clear separate choice.\r\n\r\nI would build this within the proposed marketplace release in this order: acquisition and admin publication \xe2\x86\x92 purchases and compatible selection \xe2\x86\x92 private personalization \xe2\x86\x92 performance posts/comments \xe2\x86\x92 stronger verification and comparison tools.\r\n\r\nThe first commercial decision to settle is: should the store normally acquire a non-exclusive resale licence, or purchase exclusive ownership of accepted bots?\r\n\r\nStore update:\r\nUsers can only add a bot to the store once they are running a trading session. The add to store option comes up along with the current  market details, success rate and market conditions. These details are pulled into the store to build an automated product. User is only able to dictate a cost between $30 - $50 USD and add details to the description. These are private bots that must be purchased. Because the public members-only bots may c'b'hange based on recent user experiences, the changes will need to be approved by an admin each week, reducing bot updates to approved changes weekly. \r\n\r\nWhen a purchase is made, the user gets an 80% commission.\r\n\r\nThis changes the store into a commission-based marketplace, with two distinct bot categories. I\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded the revision under the pending store proposal; it is not implemented or added to the approved roadmap yet.\r\n\r\nCategory\tAccess\tPublication and updates\r\nPaid seller bots\tPurchase required; USD $30\xe2\x80\x93$50\tProduct generated from an active trading session\r\nShared members-only bots\tAvailable to eligible members\tChanges reviewed and approved by an administrator for weekly release\r\n\r\nSession-generated paid listings\r\n\r\nThe proposed flow would be:\r\n\r\nUser runs a trading session.\r\nAdd to Store becomes available.\r\nThe app generates the product from the current bot, configuration, market, performance, and observed market conditions.\r\nThe user sets a price between $30 and $50 USD and adds description text.\r\nPublishing uploads that specific bot version as a purchase-protected package.\r\n\r\nThe generated information should include the trade count, reporting period, demo/live mode, bot version, and training status alongside its success rate. An active-session result should carry an \xe2\x80\x9cas of\xe2\x80\x9d timestamp.\r\n\r\nThe user would not manually edit the calculated success rate. A new session with one winning trade must remain distingu'b'ishable from a validated model with hundreds of trades.\r\n\r\nFreeze the version being sold\r\n\r\nThe listing should capture a fixed bot/model/configuration snapshot. Continuing to train the local bot would not silently change what customers purchase.\r\n\r\nThis also keeps customer performance reports attributable to the correct version. Uploading a listing would be an explicit exception to the private-local-storage policy\xe2\x80\x94not permission to upload the seller\xe2\x80\x99s entire history or other bots.\r\n\r\nWeekly updates for shared bots\r\n\r\nRecent user experiences can inform candidate changes, but should not directly modify the shared bot. I recommend:\r\n\r\nCollect feedback and candidate improvements.\r\nEvaluate them before the weekly review.\r\nLet an administrator approve, reject, or defer each change.\r\nPublish approved changes as a new version.\r\nKeep running sessions on their starting version.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99m interpreting your weekly restriction as applying to shared members-only bots. The approval policy for updates to paid seller bots remains to be decided.\r\n\r\nSeller commission\r\n\r\nIf the 80% is calculated from the listed sale price, the split would be:\r\n\r\nSale price\tSeller: 80%\tPlatform remainder: 20%\r\n$30\t$24\t$6\r\n$40\t$32\t$8\r\n$50\t$40\t$10\r\n\r\nThese figures are before deciding who bears payment fees, refunds, taxes, or other deductions. Commission earned and commission available for payout should also be tracked separately.\r\n\r\nThe first commercial deta'b'il to confirm: should the seller receive 80% of the gross sale price, or 80% of net proceeds after specified payment fees?\r\n\r\n80% of net price\r\n\r\nConfirmed and recorded:\r\n\r\nSeller: 80% of the net sale price.\r\nPlatform: remaining 20% of the same net price.\r\n\r\nThe deductions that define \xe2\x80\x9cnet price\xe2\x80\x9d still need agreement before payment implementation.\r\n\r\nUpdated tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nWhen a member uses a public members-only bot, it does not provide a resale option. Also, when a user does not modify a public members-only bot, the option to download does not appear. Only custom bots provide those options. \r\n\r\nThe store should also categorize bots and allow for filtering.\r\n\r\nAdditional ideas and features to discuss:\r\nThe app will keep a record of all trained user bots that have hit over 90% success rate. It will record the user, the bots success rate, the bots name, if the bot is in store and the specific market it has achieved the success in. Users will be able to filter the leaderboard by market, country/region and apps that are for sale. The app will also assign the user with the successful bot a special title in the app, specific to the marketplace, and put them on a leader board to highlight their success. Users can obtain multiple titles. This means the app needs to include a leaderboard to show users and their country/region who have created bots that hit or pass the 90% mark during thei'b"r trading session on demo or live. The public bots will also be named and ranked on the leader board.\r\n\r\nGuests will not be able to trade live or customize the basic public bots for demo trading. They will only be able to use the basic public bots in the various marketplaces. \r\n\r\nAll signed in users are able to use the basic public bots, customize the basic public bots for their own use (whether in live or demo trading), and build a bot from scratch. They will have download and upload limits for their custom bots, however, of 1 download per marketplace per day and 1 upload per day regardless of marketplace. The best place to implement this limitation may be at the browser level for efficient use of server resources. They limit also applies to the configuration file. They have unlimited access to their trading data while the browser's storage still holds the data.\r\n\r\nIf a user registers for the monthly/annual membership subscription, they will have access to basic public bots (that do not undergo training more than once annually), automatically-trained members-only public bots, unlimited downloads of their customized bots/configurations/trading data, unlimited uploads, a membership badge icon beside their names in the app and tiered max cycle lengths per month. Basic Bronze members can have a max cycle length of 7 days, Silver members can have a max cycle length of 14 days, Gold members can have a max cycle length of 21 day"b's, and Platinum members can have a maximum cycle length of 28 days. These are monthly allotments that do not roll over. Cancelled sessions still get deducted from the monthly allotment as a minimum of 1 day up to the number of days the session ran for (end date minus start date).\r\n\r\nUsers will be able to pay for each download and upload at $2.50 per download/upload. The uploads do not persist across sessions. Guest User sessions expire after 1 hour of no user interaction. They will have to sign in again and re-upload custom bot at a cost each time they want to use their downloaded custom bot if session expires or they signed out. Also, they can only trade for a maximum of 2 cycle lengths (2 days) per month. \r\n\r\nUsers can pay for each additional day after cycle lengths run out - at $0.5 per day, minimum of 2 days required. \r\n\r\nSubscribed members with customize public members-only bots - once the customized app achieves a greater success rate than what is in the server for that members-only bot, then the bot data/config/algorithm will be automatically updated on the server for all future members to benefit from, making the bots always dynamic and keeping the latest and best configurations with the latest and best success rates.\r\n\r\nThese features are feasible, but they introduce a substantial membership, billing, and permissions layer. I\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded them as CR-004 for discussion\xe2\x80\x94not yet approved or implemented.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99m i'b'nterpreting \xe2\x80\x9cper marketplace\xe2\x80\x9d as per trading market, such as EUR/USD or V100\xe2\x80\x931s, unless you mean separate stores.\r\n\r\nBot categories and permissions\r\n\r\nThe catalogue now needs three principal categories:\r\n\r\nCategory\tAvailability\tDownload/resale behaviour\r\nBasic public bots\tGuests and signed-in users\tOriginal public version has no download/resale option; trained at most annually\r\nMembers-only public bots\tSubscribers\tUnmodified original has no download/resale option; improvements follow the agreed publication process\r\nPaid custom bots\tCustomers who purchase them\tAccess and permitted use follow their licence and the user\xe2\x80\x99s plan\r\n\r\nA meaningful algorithm/configuration change should create a custom derivative, retaining its parent bot and version. A nickname alone should not unlock resale.\r\n\r\nRemoving download buttons is an application permission rule\xe2\x80\x94not absolute copy protection for algorithms executing on someone\xe2\x80\x99s device.\r\n\r\nStore filters could include market, category, compatible product, price, training status, demo/live evidence, success rate with trade count, and whether the bot is for sale.\r\n\r\nAccess rules need a clear distinction between guests and registered free users\r\n\r\nThe cleanest interpretation is:\r\n\r\nCapability\tGuest\tRegistered free user\tSubscriber\r\nUse basic public bots in demo\tYes\tYes\tYes\r\nCustomize bots/build a bot\tNo\tYes\tYes\r\nUse members-only public bots\tNo\tNo\tYes\r\nTrade live\tNo\tAfter separate aut'b'horization\tAfter separate authorization\r\nCustom bot/config downloads\tNone\tOne per market daily\tUnlimited\r\nCustom bot/config uploads\tNone\tOne daily across markets\tUnlimited\r\nAccess/export retained personal trading data\tDemo records available locally\tUnlimited\tUnlimited\r\n\r\nFor free users, I suggest bot and configuration files share the same allowance, rather than providing separate allowances for each file type.\r\n\r\nThe $2.50 charge would apply to additional eligible file operations, after included allowances are exhausted. It should not silently apply to subscribers\xe2\x80\x99 unlimited operations or to trading-data exports.\r\n\r\nYour statement about guests signing in again and paying to re-upload a custom bot conflicts with guests being unable to use custom bots. That appears better suited to registered free users.\r\n\r\nBrowser-only quota enforcement would be unreliable\r\n\r\nClearing browser storage, changing browsers, or modifying client code could reset or bypass browser counters. Prices, permissions, and billable allowances should have an authoritative server-side check; OWASP explicitly recommends deriving such business-critical values server-side.\r\n\r\nWe can still minimize server resources:\r\n\r\nKeep bot files and private datasets local.\r\nStore a small account usage ledger on the server.\r\nCache remaining allowances in the browser.\r\nAvoid duplicate charges for retries or failed operations.\r\n\r\nThese controls govern the app\xe2\x80\x99s services; 'b'they cannot prevent people copying exported files outside the app.\r\n\r\nMonthly day allowances need separating from cycle duration\r\n\r\nYour proposed figures are:\r\n\r\nMembership\tProposed monthly day figure\r\nBronze\t7 days\r\nSilver\t14 days\r\nGold\t21 days\r\nPlatinum\t28 days\r\n\r\nI recommend interpreting these as total account-wide trading-day budgets, with a separate maximum duration for one cycle.\r\n\r\nFor example, Bronze could use one seven-day cycle or several shorter cycles totalling seven days. Running five markets simultaneously would consume one account-day, rather than five.\r\n\r\nFor early cancellation, a clear proposed calculation is:\r\n\r\nCharged days = maximum of 1 and elapsed time rounded up to whole 24-hour days.\r\n\r\nThus, 29 hours consumes two days. Reserve planned days when a cycle starts, then reconcile unused days when it ends early.\r\n\r\nAdditional days would cost $0.50 each, minimum two days\xe2\x80\x94$1 minimum purchase. We still need to decide whether top-ups expire, and whether they extend the maximum individual cycle or only the remaining monthly budget.\r\n\r\nAnnual subscriptions should still receive their allowance in defined monthly periods; membership prices remain unspecified.\r\n\r\nLogin sessions, trading cycles, and local files must remain separate\r\n\r\nA one-hour inactivity expiry can be implemented, with server-side enforcement. Background price polling should not count as human interaction.\r\n\r\nHowever:\r\n\r\nExpiry must not reset 'b'a cycle\xe2\x80\x99s floor or allowance.\r\nExisting real positions cannot simply be forgotten.\r\nRe-authentication must not create another day charge for the same cycle.\r\nAn uploaded working bot can be unloaded while Option A\xe2\x80\x99s retained local history remains available.\r\nSubscribers should not pay re-upload fees where uploads are unlimited.\r\n\r\nThat preserves the distinction between losing an active file selection and losing historical records.\r\n\r\nLeaderboards and titles are possible\xe2\x80\x94but need qualification rules\r\n\r\nThe record could include the user, bot/version, market, country/region, success rate, trade count, evidence mode, dates, and sale status.\r\n\r\nI recommend:\r\n\r\nSeparate demo and live rankings.\r\nSeparate public-bot rankings from user-created-bot achievements.\r\nFilters for market, country/region, and for-sale bots.\r\nMultiple market-specific titles per user.\r\nOptional country/region disclosure.\r\nHistorical achievements labelled separately from current performance.\r\n\r\nI would use \xe2\x80\x9cat least 90%\xe2\x80\x9d consistently, subject to your confirmation.\r\n\r\nThe 500-trade training baseline does not establish a sufficiently large evaluation sample. A trained bot winning one session trade would display 100%, but should not immediately receive an expert-style title.\r\n\r\nWe therefore need a separate minimum evaluated-trade requirement for official leaderboard eligibility. Short-session milestones could remain visible as provisional achievements, 'b'with their sample sizes clearly displayed.\r\n\r\nAutomatic shared-bot replacement conflicts with weekly approval\r\n\r\nYour earlier rule required weekly administrator approval. The new rule would replace the server bot automatically whenever a customized version shows a higher rate.\r\n\r\nI recommend retaining this process:\r\n\r\nAutomatically collect candidates \xe2\x86\x92 evaluate comparable results \xe2\x86\x92 weekly admin review \xe2\x86\x92 publish an approved version.\r\n\r\nA higher rate from a different period or smaller sample is insufficient to establish an improvement. Publication should also consider net results and drawdown.\r\n\r\nThis contribution process needs a disclosed permission boundary: the relevant customized model/configuration would leave the user\xe2\x80\x99s device. Unrelated private bots and raw account histories should not be uploaded automatically.\r\n\r\nThree decisions should be settled first:\r\n\r\nDay budgets: Should 7/14/21/28 mean total monthly account-wide days, rather than a fresh allowance for every cycle?\r\nGuest/free distinction: Should paid custom-bot re-upload apply to registered free users, with guests remaining basic-demo-only\xe2\x80\x94and which group receives the two-day monthly allowance?\r\nShared-bot updates: Keep automatic candidate collection followed by weekly admin approval, or replace that policy with automatic validated publication?\r\nDay budgets: 7/14/21/28 mean total monthly account-wide days.\r\nGuest/free distinction: paid custom-bot re-'b"upload apply to registered free users, with guests remaining basic-demo-only. free users receive two-day monthly allowance.\r\nShared-bot updates: Keep automatic candidate collection followed by weekly admin approval\r\n\r\nConfirmed and recorded:\r\n\r\nPolicy\tAgreed behavior\r\nMonthly day budgets\tBronze 7, Silver 14, Gold 21, Platinum 28 total account-wide days, shared across markets and cycles. No rollover.\r\nGuests\tBasic public bots in demo only; no custom-bot uploads.\r\nRegistered free users\tTwo days per month. Paid custom-bot re-upload applies to this group.\r\nShared members-only bots\tAutomatically collect candidates; publish changes only after weekly admin approval.\r\n\r\nThese three decisions are confirmed. The remaining proposed features still await approval; no application code has changed.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nFinally, let's discuss the feasibility of these addition for these new features.\r\nSubscription members are able to recommend new members to join and if they join, the referrers receive monthly/annual passive income from their membership. \r\n\r\n25% of all membership fee goes to the referrer. The remaining comes to the business. If new member says no one referred them then a priority list of members with the least number of referees is used. The one at the top is automatically assigned in the back-end as the referrer to earn passive monthly commission income. The prior"b"ity list is limited to the active subscribed members with less than 4 referees linked to them. This creates a systematic chain of support for those who have not yet made a referral. If the referrer becomes inactive, the commission comes to the business instead until they become active again. A member can become active if they fail to pay their monthly subscription fee within the first 5 days of it being due. Commissions already paid out to the business due to inactivity will not be retro-actively paid to the member after late payment. they would have lost that month's commission. All subscribed members must have a dashboard to track their expected monthly commission, their number of referees, their referees' usernames, wallet/balance their referral link//QR and their referral username/code to share with others. Commission only applies to memberships not to the store. Members will have the option to withdraw from their wallet or have commissions automatically sent to their verified/confirmed bank account attached. If no verified wallet or account is attached, it will be logged in our own virtual custom wallet for later transfer to bank or external wallet.\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94the referral dashboard, recurring commission calculation, priority assignment and payouts are technically feasible. I recommend introducing direct referrals first, then considering automatic assignment separately. The latter changes the commercial and regulatory r"b'isk substantially.\r\n\r\nI\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded this as CR-005: Membership Referrals & Commission Payouts, pending approval.\r\n\r\nHow the commission model could work\r\n\r\nComponent\tProposed behavior\r\nAttribution\tEach paying member has one direct referrer or assigned beneficiary.\r\nCommission\tThat beneficiary receives 25% of eligible subscription payments while eligible.\r\nBusiness share\tRemaining 75%, before business expenses and any applicable payment costs.\r\nScope\tMembership subscriptions only. Store sales retain their separate commission rules.\r\nLevels\tOne level only; no commission from referrals made by someone you referred.\r\nMissing payout destination\tEarned commission remains recorded as an amount owed until a supported destination is verified.\r\n\r\nFor example, on an eligible $20 monthly payment, the member earns $5 and the business retains $15 before costs. Subscription prices remain undecided.\r\n\r\nWe must define whether the 25% applies before or after taxes, discounts, payment fees and refunds. Your earlier 80% of net price decision applies to store sales; it does not automatically define this subscription calculation.\r\n\r\nAutomatic assignment needs particular care\r\n\r\nThe system can select active subscribers with fewer than four linked members, order them by referral count, and assign the next eligible person. Ties could use the longest time since their last assignment.\r\n\r\nHowever, it cannot reliably provide four paying referrals to 'b'everyone. With 100 members, assigning each member to one beneficiary creates at most 100 relationships. Giving all 100 members four would require 400 relationships. New arrivals also become eligible beneficiaries.\r\n\r\nConsequently, this should never be advertised as guaranteed referrals, guaranteed subscription recovery or guaranteed passive income.\r\n\r\nRequiring a paid subscription to qualify for recurring rewards\xe2\x80\x94especially rewards from automatically assigned subscribers\xe2\x80\x94also deserves legal review. US FTC guidance examines recruitment incentives and purchases made to maintain compensation eligibility; having a real service does not automatically resolve those concerns. That is relevant to US expansion, not a determination of Jamaican legality.\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is to:\r\n\r\nLaunch genuine direct-referral commissions first.\r\nConsider allowing affiliate participation independently of buying a subscription.\r\nReview automatic allocation with Jamaican counsel and the selected payment provider before offering it.\r\n\r\nIf retained, call an automatically assigned recipient a commission beneficiary, rather than claiming they actually referred that customer.\r\n\r\nInactivity and annual payments need precise rules\r\n\r\nI assume \xe2\x80\x9cbecome active if they fail to pay\xe2\x80\x9d means become inactive after remaining unpaid beyond the five-day grace period.\r\n\r\nYour intended rule can be implemented: commissions arising during an ineligible period go 'b'to the business, and later renewal does not recover them. But already-earned, payable commissions should remain separately recorded; inactivity should not silently erase those balances.\r\n\r\nAnnual subscriptions introduce another decision. Paying the entire annual commission immediately makes later inactivity and refunds harder to handle. I recommend monthly vesting of annual commissions, with the dashboard distinguishing expected income from earned and withdrawable income.\r\n\r\nThe terms must also specify when eligibility is checked: at the customer\xe2\x80\x99s payment, at monthly vesting, or another defined cutoff.\r\n\r\nUse a commission balance initially\r\n\r\nAn internal commission ledger is feasible and should be stored on the server. It should distinguish:\r\n\r\nBalance\tMeaning\r\nExpected\tForecast from current subscriptions; not guaranteed\r\nPending\tRecorded but awaiting settlement or release conditions\r\nAvailable\tEarned and eligible for withdrawal\r\nProcessing\tA payout has been initiated\r\nPaid/reversed\tCompleted payments or refund adjustments\r\n\r\nA missing bank account does not turn an earned commission into business income\xe2\x80\x94it remains an unpaid obligation.\r\n\r\nI recommend starting with commission earnings and withdrawals only, without deposits, transfers between members or holding trading capital. Calling something a \xe2\x80\x9cvirtual wallet\xe2\x80\x9d does not settle its regulatory classification. BOJ identifies payment service providers as authorised e'b'ntities providing electronic payment and funds-transfer services, so the actual functionality and payout arrangement need review.\r\n\r\nBank and external-wallet payouts depend on a provider supporting your business, recipient countries, currencies and verification requirements. We have not selected one yet.\r\n\r\nThe dashboard can include all requested referral tools. Referees\xe2\x80\x99 usernames should have clear disclosure, while their banking details, email addresses and trading information remain private.\r\n\r\nBefore approving this addition, the three main decisions are:\r\n\r\nLaunch scope: direct referrals first, or retain automatic assignment for later reviewed implementation?\r\nGrace period: confirm that unpaid members become inactive after five days.\r\nCommission calculation: what deductions define the eligible fee, and should annual commissions vest monthly?\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\n\r\nDirect referrals first.\r\nUnpaid members become inactive after five days.\r\nMembership fees are collected at the start of each month. Commissions are paid 5 days after the end of the month so that refund period can be fully settled. Eligible fees then becomes 25% of the gross monthly fee, instead of the net. Annual commission should vest monthly.\r\n\r\nIf these not solve all concerns, suggest best solution for each point.\r\n\r\nThese decisions resolve the main structural questions. The remaining concerns ar'b'e refund exposure, exact eligibility timing, and payout operations. I\xe2\x80\x99ve recorded your decisions as confirmed; the recommendations below remain proposals.\r\n\r\nConfirmed policy\tBehavior\r\nReferral model\tDirect referrals first; automatic assignment deferred\r\nInactivity\tUnpaid members become inactive after five days\r\nMonthly billing\tFees collected at the start of each month\r\nCommission\t25% of the gross monthly membership fee\r\nPayment schedule\tFive days after month-end\r\nAnnual memberships\tCommission vests monthly\r\nScope\tMemberships only; store commissions remain separate\r\n\r\n1. Five days after month-end does not eliminate refund risk\r\n\r\nIt provides a useful processing buffer, but card disputes can arrive much later. Stripe documents typical dispute windows of 120 days, sometimes longer. This illustrates the risk; we have not selected Stripe as your provider.\r\n\r\nRecommended solution: Keep your payout schedule, hold disputed payments, and reverse the corresponding commission when a payment is refunded or charged back. Disclosed reversals can offset future commissions. Maintain a business-funded reserve for amounts already paid out.\r\n\r\n2. Define \xe2\x80\x9cgross fee\xe2\x80\x9d precisely\r\n\r\nMy recommendation is the subscription amount actually charged after discounts, before processing fees, excluding separately collected taxes. Refunds reduce the corresponding commission proportionately.\r\n\r\nExample\tCommission\r\n$20 monthly subscription, plus appli'b'cable tax\t$5\r\nDiscounted monthly subscription charged at $16\t$4\r\n$240 annual subscription\t$5 vested monthly; $60 across twelve months\r\n\r\nThe business absorbs processing costs from its share. Annual commission should use the actual annual subscription price, including any annual discount.\r\n\r\n3. Make billing and inactivity dates unambiguous\r\n\r\nI recommend using Jamaica time throughout:\r\n\r\nRenewal due on the 1st.\r\nGrace period through 11:59 PM on the 5th.\r\nInactive from 12:00 AM on the 6th if still unpaid.\r\nPrior-month commissions released and payouts initiated on the 5th of the following month. Bank arrival depends on processing times.\r\n\r\nFor members joining midmonth, charge a clearly disclosed prorated first month, then renew on the 1st. Annual subscribers remain active throughout their prepaid term without monthly payment requirements.\r\n\r\n4. Separate earned commissions from future eligibility\r\n\r\nTo preserve your intended forfeiture rule, I recommend:\r\n\r\nMissing the renewal cutoff forfeits that month\xe2\x80\x99s commission eligibility.\r\nLate payment restores membership access but does not recover that forfeited month.\r\nEligibility resumes in the next qualifying month.\r\nPreviously earned commissions remain payable, even if the member becomes inactive before payout.\r\n\r\nFor example, someone who earned October commission should still receive it on November 5. Missing November\xe2\x80\x99s renewal should affect November eligibility, rather than 'b'erase October earnings.\r\n\r\nNew members joining after the monthly cutoff need an explicit first-month exception, ideally earning commissions only from their activation date.\r\n\r\n5. Keep the initial \xe2\x80\x9cwallet\xe2\x80\x9d limited to commission accounting\r\n\r\nStart with an internal commission balance, supported by verified bank or payment-provider payouts. Show expected, pending, available, processing, paid and reversed amounts separately.\r\n\r\nIf no verified destination exists, available earnings remain owed to the member. Before launch, define payout minimums, currencies, fees, identity verification and failed-transfer handling. These records must remain on the server and survive logout or browser-data deletion.\r\n\r\n6. Direct referrals reduce complexity, but do not settle every legal concern\r\n\r\nThe remaining issue is requiring someone to buy membership to qualify for earnings. My preferred approach is to allow free affiliate enrollment, while paid subscriptions purchase trading-app features. This separates buying the service from earning referral commissions. It would change your proposed eligibility rule, so it requires your approval.\r\n\r\nQualified Jamaican legal review and payment-provider acceptance should precede launch. Referral income should be marketed as variable earnings from genuine subscription sales, with no guaranteed return.\r\n\r\nThese recommendations give you a workable foundation without changing your confirmed 25% gross comm'b"ission or monthly payout schedule.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nNow, let's go through the change request for approvals\r\n\r\nWe\xe2\x80\x99ll review one change request at a time. For each, you can approve, approve with changes, defer or reject. Approval adds it to the implementation backlog; it does not activate payments or live trading.\r\n\r\nOrder\tChange request\r\n1\tCR-001: Accounts, Security & Profiles\r\n2\tCR-002: Private Local Data, Bot Files & Exports\r\n3\tCR-003: Bot Store & Seller Commissions\r\n4\tCR-004: Memberships, Usage Limits & Leaderboards\r\n5\tCR-005: Membership Referrals & Commission Payouts\r\n\r\nCR-001 \xe2\x80\x94 Accounts, Security & Profiles\r\n\r\nThe proposed scope is:\r\n\r\nFeature\tBehavior for approval\r\nRegistration\tVerified email/password, Google sign-in or GitHub sign-in\r\nAccount recovery\tPassword reset and email verification through expiring, single-use links\r\nTwo-factor authentication\tAuthenticator app with recovery codes; optional for demo, required before live trading and designated sensitive actions\r\nProfile personalization\tEdit avatar, display name and unique username during an active session\r\nAccount linking\tConnect additional sign-in methods after authenticating the existing account\r\nUser separation\tAccount ownership checks protect private settings, bots, history and Deriv connections\r\nDeveloper testing\tStartup-controlled authentication bypass using a separate test identity; visib"b'le development indicator; live trading blocked\r\nProduction protection\tAuthentication bypass cannot run in production\r\nSession handling\tSecure login/logout; session expiry cannot erase or transfer responsibility for existing broker positions\r\n\r\nGoogle sign-in requests identity access only. GitHub sign-in does not request repository access.\r\n\r\nProposed priority: High\xe2\x80\x94implement the account foundation before the multi-user Bot Manager, memberships and store. Final version numbering will follow this approval review.\r\n\r\nAcceptance: Registration, verification, recovery, profile editing and two-factor flows work; cross-user access is denied; developer bypass cannot enable live trading or production access.\r\n\r\nDo you approve CR-001 as described, approve with changes, defer it or reject it?\r\n\r\napprove with one addition - the user should be able to enter and update their contact information, and choose their country/region. They should also have the option to disable if they want to be displayed on the leader board for their custom bots performance.\r\n\r\nCR-001 is approved with your additions and recorded:\r\n\r\nUsers can enter and update contact information.\r\nUsers can select and update their country/region.\r\nUsers can disable public leaderboard display of their custom-bot performance.\r\n\r\nContact details remain private. The leaderboard preference will control public entries without deleting private performance history. I recommend lead'b'erboard visibility off by default; that default remains proposed.\r\n\r\nNext: CR-002 \xe2\x80\x94 Private Local Data, Bot Files & Exports\r\n\r\nFeature\tBehavior for approval\r\nLocal history\tAutomatically retain private, account-separated trading/training history on the device\xe2\x80\x94your previously confirmed Option A\r\nData downloads\tExport retained account data for selected dates, markets and modes as CSV or XLSX, including an active-session snapshot\r\nPersonal bot files\tImport and export full bots, configurations and learned updates\r\nSave/discard\tAt trading/training session end, offer saving a new bot version or discarding bot changes; discarding preserves trading history\r\nFile location\tUse normal browser downloads, or a supported user-authorized file/folder picker\r\nServer preferences\tExplicitly saved market configurations persist separately from local bot files\r\nPublic bots\tOnly admins publish or update the public catalog; customizing one creates a private copy\r\nRecovery\tAutomatically retain a minimal local journal of cycle protections and pending/open orders so crashes or discarded changes cannot reset trading obligations\r\nPrivacy\tExports exclude credentials and live-capital authorization; private training runs locally or through the local Python application\r\nRetention controls\tProvide local-storage usage, deletion controls and clear warnings that deleted or unavailable history cannot be exported\r\n\r\nServer boundary: Authentication, profiles,'b' security records and saved market preferences remain server-side. Store assets, subscriptions, commissions and leaderboard submissions require their separately approved server records.\r\n\r\nClosing a browser cannot reliably trigger a save prompt, so the app will provide an explicit End Session action. Signing in elsewhere retrieves server preferences, not private files left on another device.\r\n\r\nProposed priority: High\xe2\x80\x94establish this before expanding Bot Manager, training and portable files. Any download/upload quotas will be reviewed under CR-004.\r\n\r\nAcceptance: Account separation works; exports match retained records and selected dates; save/discard behaves correctly; restarting preserves risk controls and order reconciliation.\r\n\r\nDo you approve CR-002 as described, approve with changes, defer it or reject it?\r\n\r\napprove\r\n\r\nCR-002 is approved and recorded. CR-001 and CR-002 are now approved for implementation; neither has been implemented yet.\r\n\r\nNext: CR-003 \xe2\x80\x94 Bot Store & Seller Commissions\r\n\r\nFeature\tScope for approval\r\nCreate a listing\tAvailable during an active trading session; capture an immutable copy of the current custom bot and configuration\r\nAutomated product details\tInclude market, bot version, market-condition snapshot, calculated win rate, sample size, demo/live source, period, profit and measured drawdown\r\nSeller inputs\tSet price between US$30\xe2\x80\x93$50 and add descriptive text; cannot manually replace calcu'b'lated statistics\r\nPrivate paid products\tStore the listed package on the server; require purchase entitlement to access it\r\nSeller commission\t80% of net sale price; business receives the remaining 20% of that same base\r\nPurchased bots\tShow compatible purchases in the market\xe2\x80\x99s bot selector; allow private nicknames and descriptions\r\nCustomer results\tPreview and publish session statistics with comments; distinguish original and customized versions and demo/live results\r\nStore discovery\tCategorize and filter by market, bot type, price, training status and performance evidence\r\nPublic-bot restrictions\tUnmodified members-only public bots offer no download or resale action\r\nLicensing\tA custom bot\xe2\x80\x99s download/resale availability remains subject to ownership and derivative-use permissions\r\nShared-bot updates\tAutomatically collect candidates, then publish only after weekly admin approval\r\nVersion protection\tExisting sessions retain their selected version; published updates do not silently alter running bots\r\n\r\nRecommended decisions to complete this request\r\n\r\nListing eligibility: Allow both live-price demo and real-money sessions, clearly labelled. Offline simulated results cannot be presented as market-trading evidence.\r\nNet sale price: Define it as the actual sale amount excluding taxes, less payment-processing fees and applicable refunds/chargebacks. Show each deduction in the seller\xe2\x80\x99s ledger.\r\nPaid-bot updates: Require admin'b' review before publication. Customers retain their purchased version and explicitly choose whether to install an approved update.\r\nPrivate purchase labels: Store nicknames/descriptions as private account preferences so they follow the buyer across devices.\r\nPayout rules: Finalize seller payout timing, minimums and refund handling alongside the payment system. The membership commission schedule does not automatically apply to store sales.\r\n\r\nPublishing a listing explicitly uploads that bot package. Posting results uploads the selected performance summary\xe2\x80\x94not unrelated private history or credentials. Published win rates describe recorded results and do not promise future performance.\r\n\r\nProposed placement: Expand the existing marketplace release after accounts, bot versioning, performance records and purchase entitlements are ready.\r\n\r\nAcceptance: Listings preserve the correct bot version; purchases enforce access; commission calculations reconcile; public-bot restrictions work; reports cannot double-count trades; updates require approval.\r\n\r\nDo you approve CR-003, including the five recommended decisions above, approve with changes, defer it or reject it?\r\n\r\napproved\r\n\r\nCR-003 is approved and recorded, including its five recommended decisions. CR-001\xe2\x80\x93CR-003 are now approved for implementation.\r\n\r\nNext: CR-004 \xe2\x80\x94 Memberships, Usage Limits & Leaderboards\r\n\r\nAccount type\tAccess and allowances\r\nGuest\tBasic public bots in d'b'emo only; no customization, custom-file uploads or live trading; expires after one hour without interaction\r\nRegistered free\tBasic public bots, customization and building bots; live trading requires separate authorization; two trading days monthly\r\nSubscriber\tFree-account features plus members-only public bots, membership badge and unlimited custom-bot/configuration uploads/downloads\r\nAll registered users\tUnlimited access/export of locally retained trading data\r\n\r\nBasic public bots undergo training no more than annually. Members-only public bots receive approved updates through the weekly review process.\r\n\r\nSubscription\tTotal monthly account-wide trading days\r\nBronze\t7\r\nSilver\t14\r\nGold\t21\r\nPlatinum\t28\r\n\r\nThese allowances are shared across markets and cycles and do not roll over. Subscription prices remain undecided.\r\n\r\nUsage rules proposed for approval\r\n\r\nFree accounts receive one combined bot/configuration download per trading market per day, and one combined upload daily across all markets.\r\nAdditional bot/configuration operations cost US$2.50 each, after included allowances are used. Subscribers incur no such charges.\r\nTrading-data exports remain unlimited and uncharged. I recommend treating raw CSV training-data imports separately from paid bot/configuration imports.\r\nFailed operations and retries do not produce duplicate charges.\r\nQuotas, payments and permissions are enforced on the server; the browser displays remain'b'ing allowances.\r\nUploaded working bots do not automatically persist across sessions. Re-upload uses an available allowance or paid operation; retained local history remains intact.\r\nAdditional trading days cost US$0.50 each, purchased in blocks of at least two days.\r\n\r\nRecommended day-accounting decisions\r\n\r\nReset allowances on the 1st of each month, Jamaica time, including annual subscribers.\r\nUse one active account-wide cycle covering all selected markets; five simultaneous markets consume the same cycle days.\r\nReserve planned days at cycle start. Cancellation consumes at least one day, rounded up by elapsed 24-hour periods: 29 hours consumes two days. Release unused reservations.\r\nExtra days expire at month-end and do not roll over. A cycle must fit the remaining days before that reset; purchased days can extend its available duration within that boundary.\r\nLogin expiry does not restart a cycle or reset risk controls. Exhausted allowances stop new entries while outstanding positions remain managed.\r\n\r\nLeaderboard and titles\r\n\r\nThe proposed scope includes named public bots and consenting users\xe2\x80\x99 custom bots, market-specific titles, multiple titles per user, and filters for market, country/region and sale status.\r\n\r\nFor consistent qualification, I recommend:\r\n\r\nAt least 90% win rate, inclusive.\r\nA separate minimum of 500 eligible settled forward-demo or live trades for the exact bot/configuration version and market\xe2\x80\x94not'b' merely 500 training records.\r\nSeparate demo/live rankings and public/custom-bot categories.\r\nDisplay sample size, evaluation period, net profit, drawdown and evidence level.\r\nPreserve historical achievements while distinguishing them from current performance.\r\nHonor the CR-001 visibility preference; recommend off by default.\r\nCompare shared-bot candidates on compatible evaluation data before weekly admin approval; a higher percentage alone does not trigger publication.\r\n\r\nThese thresholds qualify a recorded achievement, not a profitability or safety guarantee.\r\n\r\nProposed placement: Build entitlements before paid usage; add leaderboards after reliable version-specific performance records. Membership prices remain a separate decision.\r\n\r\nDo you approve CR-004, including these recommended rules, approve with changes, defer it or reject it?\r\n\r\napprove\r\n\r\nCR-004 is approved and recorded, including its recommended rules. CR-001\xe2\x80\x93CR-004 are now approved for implementation.\r\n\r\nFinal review: CR-005 \xe2\x80\x94 Membership Referrals & Commission Payouts\r\n\r\nFeature\tScope for approval\r\nReferral model\tDirect referrals only initially; automatic assignment deferred\r\nEligible referrers\tActive subscribed members, preserving your requested eligibility rule\r\nCommission\t25% of gross membership fees; no commission on store purchases or usage top-ups\r\nMonthly billing\tRenewals due on the 1st\r\nGrace period\tThrough 11:59 PM on the 5th, Jamaica time; ina'b'ctive from the 6th if unpaid\r\nCommission payment\tPrior-month commissions released and payouts initiated on the 5th of the following month\r\nAnnual membership\tCommission vests monthly using the actual annual subscription price\r\nDashboard\tReferral link, QR, code, referred usernames/count, expected earnings, pending/available balances and payout history\r\nWithdrawals\tRequested withdrawals or automatic payouts to a verified, supported destination\r\nMissing destination\tAvailable commission remains recorded as owed until a destination is verified\r\n\r\nRecommended rules included for approval\r\n\r\nGross calculation: Use the subscription amount actually charged after discounts, before processing fees, excluding separately collected taxes. Refunds reduce commission proportionately. A $240 annual subscription produces $60 total commission, vesting at $5 per service month.\r\nLate-payment eligibility: Missing the grace deadline forfeits that month\xe2\x80\x99s unvested referral commission. Late payment restores membership access, but commission eligibility resumes in the next qualifying month. Previously earned commissions remain payable.\r\nMidmonth registration: Prorate the first subscription month. New referrers qualify from paid activation for that initial month; they cannot earn on earlier transactions.\r\nAnnual status: Annual subscribers remain active throughout their prepaid term. Allocate monthly vesting to the applicable service periods, with doc'b'umented rounding and partial-month handling.\r\nRefund protection: Hold disputed commissions, reverse commissions attributable to refunds/chargebacks, and offset disclosed reversals against future earnings. Maintain a business reserve for exposure after payout.\r\nAttribution: One direct referrer per subscriber, fixed at first paid activation. Show the selected referrer before confirmation; prevent self-referrals and duplicate-account abuse.\r\nFinancial records: Maintain an authoritative server-side commission ledger. Browser deletion, logout or inactivity cannot erase earned balances.\r\nPayout scope: Start with commission accounting and disbursements\xe2\x80\x94not deposits, transfers between users or custody of trading funds. Finalize provider, supported countries/currencies, minimums and fees before launch.\r\n\r\nLaunch condition: Subscriber-only earning eligibility remains subject to qualified legal review and payment-provider acceptance. Free affiliate enrollment remains an alternative for later consideration; it is not silently substituted into this request.\r\n\r\nProposed placement: After subscriptions, identity verification and payment reconciliation. Bank arrival times may follow the payout initiation date.\r\n\r\nAcceptance: Accurate attribution, gross-fee calculations, monthly vesting, inactivity handling, refund adjustments and reconciled payouts, with no duplicate payments.\r\n\r\nDo you approve CR-005 as described, approve with changes, 'b'defer it or reject it?\r\n\r\nAccept with these changes - Members do not become eligible for commission until after completing three (3) months of uninterrupted membership. They also do not receive commission for referees who have not pass this 3 month probation.\r\n\r\nCR-005 is approved with your changes and recorded.\r\n\r\nCommission eligibility now requires both:\r\n\r\nThe referrer completing three months of uninterrupted membership.\r\nThe referred member completing their own three-month probation.\r\n\r\nProbation-period fees generate no commission or later backpay. Annual prepayment does not bypass the elapsed three-month requirement. The dashboard will show each person\xe2\x80\x99s qualification progress.\r\n\r\nFor implementation, I\xe2\x80\x99ll interpret \xe2\x80\x9cuninterrupted\xe2\x80\x9d as continuity through the existing five-day grace period; becoming inactive resets that person\xe2\x80\x99s qualifying clock. Commission begins only for eligible service periods after both qualify.\r\n\r\nAll five change requests are now approved. Implementation and testing remain pending; release numbering will be updated around the approved scope.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nAdd a requirement for an Admin panel with role-based access levels for reviewing financials, memberships, payouts, membership conversations, disputes, etc. Full Admins and Membership Managers should access to flag or block a customer. All other admin-related tasks for the 'b'business should be included for the relevant employees to access. Employee accounts will need to be created and employee database created. Full Admins will be able to deactivate, suspend and re-activate employees, as well as modify access levels.\r\n\r\nAdded CR-006 \xe2\x80\x94 Admin Panel, Employee Accounts & Role-Based Access as an approved requirement. Implementation remains pending.\r\n\r\nThe panel will cover financials, memberships, referrals, payouts, member-support conversations, disputes, store moderation, bot approvals, usage allowances, leaderboards and audit records.\r\n\r\nRole\tMain access\r\nFull Admin\tPlatform oversight; create employee accounts; modify access; suspend, deactivate and reactivate employees; flag/block customers\r\nMembership Manager\tMemberships, eligibility, member-support cases; flag/block customers\r\nFinance/Accounts\tRevenue, fees, commissions, reconciliation and financial reports\r\nPayout Operations\tReview and process payouts within assigned approval permissions\r\nSupport\tMember conversations, case notes and escalation\r\nDisputes/Compliance\tDisputes, evidence, investigations and authorized resolutions\r\nMarketplace/Bot Reviewer\tListings, seller submissions and bot-update approvals\r\nAnalyst/Auditor\tScoped, read-only reporting and audit access\r\n\r\nEmployees can hold multiple roles. Permissions will distinguish view, edit, approve, execute and export.\r\n\r\nThe employee database will retain identity, work contact details, ro'b'les, account status and status history. Full Admin changes will be audited; suspension or deactivation will revoke active staff access.\r\n\r\nAdditional controls include:\r\n\r\nIndividual staff accounts, MFA and server-enforced permissions.\r\nRecorded reasons for customer flags, blocks and employee-status changes.\r\nProtection against removing the last active Full Admin.\r\nCustomer blocking preserves financial obligations and safe management of open trades.\r\nAdmin access does not expose private files stored only on a customer\xe2\x80\x99s device.\r\n\r\nI recommend separating payout preparation from approval for higher-risk transactions; thresholds remain to be defined.\r\n\r\nPriority: Establish staff accounts and access controls after authentication, before launching paid operations. Deliver each admin module alongside its corresponding business feature.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nAlso, include a fun, attractive, professional landing page. In the admin section, allow for content management - where business name is set and the content and company details on the app can be modified. \r\n\r\nThe necessary dummy member accounts should also be created with relevant restrictions to model user experience and user testing.\r\n\r\nAdded as CR-007 \xe2\x80\x94 Landing Page, Content Management & Test Accounts, with content-management permissions extending CR-006.\r\n\r\nLanding page\r\n\r\nA fun, attractive, professional design w'b'ith mobile support, product introduction, demo/sign-up actions, features, membership comparison, FAQs and contact information. Performance claims will use genuine evidence.\r\n\r\nAdmin content management\r\n\r\nAuthorized employees can manage:\r\n\r\nBusiness name, logo, tagline and company description.\r\nContact details and social links.\r\nLanding-page sections, FAQs, help content and announcements.\r\n\r\nA Content Editor role can edit and preview drafts; authorized publishers can publish or restore previous versions. Content editing cannot change trading protections, permissions or financial rules.\r\n\r\nDummy accounts and testing\r\n\r\nTest coverage\tIncluded personas\r\nAccess levels\tGuest, registered free, Bronze, Silver, Gold and Platinum\r\nMembership states\tNew, grace period, inactive, blocked and allowance exhausted\r\nReferrals\tProbationary, qualified, interrupted membership and annual prepaid\r\nStore\tBuyer, seller, customized bot and restricted public bot\r\nLeaderboards\tVisibility disabled, enabled and qualifying performance\r\nEmployees\tEach admin role, plus suspended/deactivated accounts\r\n\r\nTest accounts will include clearly labelled sample data, expected permissions and a repeatable reset process. They will be isolated from real payments, payouts and live trading.\r\n\r\nThese requirements are approved and recorded; the page, CMS and accounts have not yet been implemented.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDoc'b"ument\r\nNow, let's review all outstanding approval requests.\r\n\r\nAll seven change requests are approved. No feature-level approval requests remain outstanding. What remains is a set of business-policy decisions and launch arrangements.\r\n\r\nChange request\tStatus\r\nCR-001 \xe2\x80\x94 Accounts, Security & Profiles\tApproved with additions\r\nCR-002 \xe2\x80\x94 Private Data, Bot Files & Exports\tApproved\r\nCR-003 \xe2\x80\x94 Bot Store & Seller Commissions\tApproved\r\nCR-004 \xe2\x80\x94 Memberships, Limits & Leaderboards\tApproved\r\nCR-005 \xe2\x80\x94 Referrals & Commission Payouts\tApproved with three-month qualification\r\nCR-006 \xe2\x80\x94 Admin Panel & Employee Access\tApproved\r\nCR-007 \xe2\x80\x94 Landing Page, Content Management & Test Accounts\tApproved\r\n\r\nSome older tracker entries describe proposals as pending; the later approval records supersede them.\r\n\r\nThe remaining decisions are:\r\n\r\nItem\tDecision still needed\tRecommended approach\r\n1. Membership pricing\tMonthly and annual prices for Bronze, Silver, Gold and Platinum; annual discounts\tSet prices after estimating operating costs, payment fees and referral commissions\r\n2. Bot ownership and resale\tWhat buyers may modify/resell; what qualifies as a custom derivative; rights for customized public bots\tPersonal use/customization by default; resale only under an explicit licence. Renaming alone does not qualify\r\n3. Payout arrangements\tProvider, supported countries/currencies, withdrawal minimums, fees and seller payout schedule\tUse verified prov"b'ider-managed payouts. Keep store and membership earnings separately identifiable\r\n4. Refunds and subscription changes\tRefund periods; annual cancellations; upgrades/downgrades; treatment of unused days and file credits\tPublish clear rules before payment. Show the charge, allowance adjustment and effective date before confirmation\r\n5. Financial approval controls\tWho may approve refunds/payouts; thresholds requiring a second approver; small-team fallback\tSeparate preparation from approval where staffing permits; audit any permitted exception\r\n6. Weekly bot-review schedule\tSubmission cutoff, review day and publication time\tEstablish one weekly review window; publish only approved versions and retain rollback\r\n7. Referral boundary cases\tCommission treatment when either party completes probation partway through a month; annual partial periods and rounding\tBegin accrual only after both qualifying timestamps; prorate eligible service periods, with no probation backpay\r\n8. Branding and company details\tBusiness/app name, logo, company description, support contacts and public company information\tUse clearly labelled placeholders for testing until supplied\r\n\r\nImplementation planning also remains to be finalized: revised release numbering, hosting/deployment arrangement and the final employee permission matrix. Routine technical choices do not need another feature-approval round.\r\n\r\nLaunch gates remain separate from your approvals: le'b'gal/payment-provider review for the referral model, genuine authentication/payment integration checks, and explicit authorization before real-money trading. These do not prevent building or testing the demo.\r\n\r\nUser acceptance of the delivered v1.1 also remains unreported; that is a testing status, not an outstanding scope approval.\r\n\r\nI suggest we resolve bot ownership/resale rights and referral boundary cases first, because they determine application behavior. Pricing, payout-provider selection and final branding can remain configurable while development proceeds.\r\n\r\nWho will be able to add employees?\r\nWho can sign up a new member?\r\n\r\nEmployees: Only Full Admins can create or invite employees, assign access levels, suspend/deactivate them, and reactivate them. Membership Managers cannot add employees.\r\n\r\nMembers: Anyone can register through the public sign-up page using verified email/password, Google or GitHub. A referral link/code can attribute the registration, but the referrer cannot create or control the new member\xe2\x80\x99s account.\r\n\r\nStaff-assisted member registration has not yet been included. I recommend allowing Membership Managers and Full Admins to send registration invitations, with the member completing verification and accepting the terms themselves.\r\n\r\nMembership Managers and Full Admins can send registration invitations, with the member completing verification and accepting the terms themselves. This can be d'b'one via the admin panel.\r\n\r\nAdded to CR-006 as an approved requirement.\r\n\r\nMembership Managers and Full Admins can send, resend, revoke and track registration invitations through the admin panel.\r\n\r\nInvitees must complete their own verification and accept the terms. An invitation does not activate a paid subscription or authorize live trading.\r\n\r\nEmployee creation remains Full Admin only. Implementation is pending.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nRequire security validation for membership account management, noting in the panel when security bypass occurs with employee reasons necessary. Every membership account access must include notes and reason for access along with outcome. It should fall within expected categories or user inputed in reason is outside the expected categories.\r\n\r\nAdded to CR-006 as an approved requirement covering every staff access to a member account, including read-only access.\r\n\r\nStage\tRequired information/control\r\nBefore access\tValidate employee identity, permissions and required security checks\r\nAccess reason\tSelect a category and enter explanatory notes\r\nOther reason\tRequire a written explanation outside the predefined categories\r\nDuring access\tLog account views, changes, exports and actions against the employee\r\nCompletion\tRequire outcome category and outcome notes\r\nInterrupted access\tMark incomplete and flag for follow-up\r\n\r\nInitial reason catego'b'ries include registration/verification, profile updates, subscriptions/billing, referrals, payouts, disputes, technical support, security/fraud, compliance and customer restrictions.\r\n\r\nSecurity exceptions will appear prominently in the panel, recording the affected check, employee, justification, approval where required, timestamp and outcome. Entering a reason alone does not authorize a bypass.\r\n\r\nAudit records cannot be silently overwritten; corrections remain linked amendments. This is separate from the developer-only testing bypass.\r\n\r\nImplementation remains pending.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nNow, create a detailed design portfolio that shows graphical mockups of each page for each process.\r\n\r\nCreated 89 graphical page mockups across 20 process walkthroughs, covering member, trading, store and admin experiences.\r\n\r\n105-page design portfolio PDF\r\nInteractive portfolio \xe2\x80\x94 download and open in your browser; includes search, linked workflows and mobile preview.\r\nEditable source package\r\n\r\nIncludes security validation, mandatory access reasons/outcomes, employee permissions, referral probation and restricted-account states.\r\n\r\nTradecraft is a temporary brand. These are design mockups with fictional data, not implemented trading or payment features.\r\n\r\ntrading-platform-design-portfolio.pdf\r\nPDF\r\ntrading-platform-design-portfolio.html\r\nHTML\r\nCreate a relevant, fun, indus'b'try-specific, catchy business/app name coined from some aspect of this: "LB27 Trading Bot Commission Workshop"\r\n\r\nLB27 BotForge\r\n\r\nA catchy name combining LB27, trading bots, and the workshop idea\xe2\x80\x94a place where users build, train, refine and share their trading strategies.\r\n\r\nTagline: Build your bot. Forge your strategy.\r\n\r\nIt fits the trading workspace, bot marketplace and member community without making commissions the entire brand.\r\n\r\nOther strong options:\r\n\r\nLB27 TradeCraft \xe2\x80\x94 emphasizes strategy and skill.\r\nBotWorks27 \xe2\x80\x94 playful, memorable and workshop-inspired.\r\nLB27 Botique \xe2\x80\x94 \xe2\x80\x9cbot\xe2\x80\x9d + \xe2\x80\x9cboutique,\xe2\x80\x9d ideal for the marketplace.\r\n\r\nMy pick: LB27 BotForge. Name and trademark availability have not been checked.\r\n\r\nBotForge27\r\n\r\nBotForge27 is confirmed as the selected app name and recorded for future designs and development.\r\n\r\nSuggested tagline: Build your bot. Forge your strategy.\r\n\r\nNew feature:\r\nCreate a space where members can create their own video content channel, specifying free, pay-per-view or channel subscription. Guests and members are able to view all free content. Guests and members are also able to view pay/subscribe to members\' content. Content will also be featured at the top based on views, favorites, popularity, recency, etc. Creators can also link their youtube, tiktok and instagram content (videos, shorts, reels or full youtube channel) to one of their app channels for full integration. Thes'b'e linked content cannot be marked as pay-per-view or require a paid subscription. Viewing and subscription is free. Only members (free or subscription) can subscribe, like or comment on video content. Only directly uploaded or directly recorded content may be monetized.\r\n\r\nAdded CR-008 \xe2\x80\x94 Creator Video Channels & Monetization to BotForge27\xe2\x80\x99s requirements. It is feasible, with a few access and commercial details to resolve.\r\n\r\nChannel and content rules\r\n\r\nFeature\tRequirement\r\nCreate channels\tRegistered free and subscribed members\r\nFree content\tViewable by guests and members\r\nNative videos\tDirect uploads or recordings can be free, pay-per-view or subscriber-only\r\nExternal content\tLinked YouTube, TikTok and Instagram content always remains free\r\nEngagement\tOnly registered members can follow, like or comment\r\nDiscovery\tFeatured content based on views, favourites, popularity and recency\r\nCreator dashboard\tManage channels, content, pricing, analytics and earnings\r\nAdmin controls\tContent reports, moderation, rights complaints, disputes and financial oversight\r\n\r\nA channel can contain both paid native videos and free external videos. Its paid subscription must never block access to those external videos.\r\n\r\nExternal integration is possible, within platform limits\r\n\r\nYouTube supports embedded players and playlists; its channel metadata can support listing channel uploads. TikTok supports video and creator-profile embeds. Instagr'b'am supports public-content embedding. These integrations do not automatically synchronize external likes, comments or subscribers.\r\n\r\nUnavailable, deleted or private content will need an appropriate fallback. Linked content will retain its source attribution.\r\n\r\nTwo decisions need clarification\r\n\r\nGuest subscriptions: You said guests can purchase subscriptions, but also that only members can subscribe. I recommend distinguishing:\r\nFollow: Free, registered members only.\r\nPaid channel subscription: Requires at least a free registered account.\r\nPay-per-view: Guests may purchase through secure checkout, with a receipt/access-restoration method.\r\nCreator revenue: What percentage should creators receive? The existing 80% of net bot sales and 25% membership referral commission will not automatically apply to video sales.\r\n\r\nVideo pricing, purchase-access duration and hosting limits also need defining. Unlimited bot-file operations will remain separate from video storage and streaming allowances.\r\n\r\nThis feature adds a substantial media module. I recommend delivering free channels and external embeds first, followed by native uploads/recording and monetization.\r\n\r\nThe current design portfolio does not yet include these new pages.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nEmployees must be able to review past membership interactions, with conversation history, reasons, actions and outcome. Empl'b'oyees must be able to set follow-ups and escalate to other employees. Automatic reminders should be set for follow-ups and notifications along with inbox message for all new activities. Employees must have a tasks view and inbox that shows all tasks for them to review and update, along with inbox to see all updates and to share messages among employee team. This is in the admin only section.\r\n\r\nAdded to CR-006 as an approved admin-only Employee Workspace requirement.\r\n\r\nArea\tIncluded behavior\r\nMember interaction history\tConversations, access reasons, notes, security checks/exceptions, actions, outcomes and responsible employees\r\nFollow-ups\tAssign an owner, due date/time, priority and notes; reschedule or complete with an outcome\r\nEscalations\tSend to an authorized employee/team with context, reason and requested action; track acceptance and resolution\r\nMy Tasks\tAssigned work grouped by today, upcoming, overdue, waiting and completed\r\nEmployee Inbox\tActivity updates, reminders, assignments and internal team-message threads\r\nNotifications\tAutomatically notify relevant employees of new activities and due/overdue follow-ups\r\n\r\nReminders will run even when the employee\xe2\x80\x99s browser is closed. Reading an inbox message will not mark its task complete.\r\n\r\nAccess remains role-scoped. Reviewing historical member records still requires security validation, an access reason and outcome. Escalation does not automatically grant additional'b' permissions.\r\n\r\nUnfinished work assigned to suspended/deactivated employees will enter an authorized reassignment queue.\r\n\r\nImplementation and updated graphical mockups remain pending.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nThe app must be very simplified, attractive, fun, intuitive/easy to use, and user-friendly.\r\n\r\nConfirmed\xe2\x80\x94this is a design requirement across all BotForge27 pages, including the admin panel.\r\n\r\nSimple: Show essentials first; place advanced settings behind clearly labelled options.\r\nIntuitive: Use plain language, consistent navigation and obvious next steps.\r\nAttractive and fun: Use polished visuals, friendly copy and subtle animation without distracting from decisions.\r\nEasy to complete: Keep forms short, reuse known information and guide complex processes step by step.\r\nClear feedback: Explain progress, errors, restrictions and successful actions.\r\nAccessible: Support mobile screens, keyboard navigation, readable text and strong contrast.\r\nResponsible: Make costs, trading risks and confirmations clear; avoid incentives that encourage excessive trading.\r\n\r\nMember screens will focus on the task at hand. Admin screens will prioritize My Tasks, Inbox and relevant actions, with detailed records available when needed.\r\n\r\nmore technical users must have an option for more granular bot configurations.\r\n\r\nAdded: BotForge27 will offer Simple and Advanced configuration mo'b"des.\r\n\r\nMode\tExperience\r\nSimple \xe2\x80\x94 default\tGuided setup, presets, essential settings and plain-language explanations\r\nAdvanced \xe2\x80\x94 optional\tGranular, market-specific algorithm parameters, signal filters, entry/exit rules, timing, sizing and training settings supported by the selected bot\r\n\r\nAdvanced mode will include parameter descriptions, valid ranges, configuration validation, comparison with defaults, and save/import/export options.\r\n\r\nShared risk protections remain mandatory in both modes. Advanced settings cannot override allocation limits, the protected floor, combined exposure or live-trading authorization. Performance-relevant changes create a distinct bot version.\r\n\r\nWhat's the next version to build?\r\n\r\nThe next build is BotForge27 v1.2 \xe2\x80\x94 Market Workspace, extending the delivered v1.1.\r\n\r\nIt will include:\r\n\r\nEight-market selection, with up to five selected simultaneously.\r\nMini-graphs in a row, with abbreviated market names.\r\nClick a name or mini-graph to open its larger observation chart.\r\nClear market availability, source freshness and trading eligibility.\r\nEvidence-based suggestions when sufficient current data exists.\r\nWatch-only status for markets requiring an execution adapter.\r\nBotForge27 branding and a simpler, polished interface.\r\n\r\nThe granular Advanced bot editor comes with Bot Manager/configuration work. Accounts and admin foundations follow before multi-user paid features.\r\n\r\nCurrent status: v1.2 "b'is planned, not started. Your v1.1 testing results are still unreported.\r\n\r\nversion 1.1 report - The market observation graph need data labels and horizontal axis label. Also, it needs a way to override the pause once floor is reached. The user should be able to manually confirm over-riding the floor to restart with new balance and calculated floor for new set capital. Also, individual capital should be assigned for each marketplace trader initialized.\r\n\r\nRecorded your v1.1 testing feedback as changes requested.\r\n\r\n1. Observation graph \xe2\x80\x94 correction for v1.2\r\n\r\nAdd price/data labels, a horizontal-axis label, and readable time ticks with timezone. Existing observations without timestamps must be labelled honestly rather than assigned invented times.\r\n\r\n2. Floor-stop restart \xe2\x80\x94 proposed behavior\r\n\r\nWhen a trader reaches its floor:\r\n\r\nStop new entries and reconcile outstanding orders.\r\nOffer \xe2\x80\x9cRestart with new capital.\xe2\x80\x9d\r\nShow available balance, requested allocation, recalculated floor and new loss allowance.\r\nRequire explicit confirmation.\r\nPreserve the previous run\xe2\x80\x99s losses and floor in history.\r\n\r\nThe old floor remains unchanged; the newly authorized run receives its own floor.\r\n\r\nRepeated resets increase cumulative risk: $50 stopping at $40, then restarting that $40 with a $32 floor, permits a total loss of $18, exceeding the original $10 allowance.\r\n\r\n3. Individual market allocations \xe2\x80\x94 proposed behavior\r\n\r\nEach i'b'nitialized market trader gets its own capital allocation, balance, floor, profit targets and trading status. Combined allocations cannot exceed available account funds. One market reaching its floor pauses that trader.\r\n\r\nMonthly trading-day allowances remain account-wide; restarting capital does not replenish days.\r\n\r\nThe reset and per-market allocations change the approved risk model. Under your change-control instruction, please confirm adding these as CR-009 to v1.2. The chart correction is already recorded.\r\n\r\nUpdated implementation tracker\r\n\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nbuild v1.2\r\n\r\nBotForge27 v1.2 is built and ready for testing.\r\n\r\nDownload BotForge27 v1.2\r\n\r\nIncludes:\r\n\r\nEight-market lineup, five selected mini-graphs and expanded observation.\r\nPrice labels, time axis, timestamps and hover/tap details.\r\nIndividual trader capital, balances and protective floors.\r\nExplicit floor-stop restart that preserves previous losses and cycle expiry.\r\nArchived trader runs and CSV exports.\r\n\r\nTo start on Windows:\r\n\r\nStop v1.1 and back up its data folder.\r\nExtract the ZIP and open the BotForge27 folder.\r\nRun start-live-demo.bat with Python 3.11+ installed.\r\nOpen http://127.0.0.1:8765.\r\n\r\nThe README explains migrating existing data. Default fictional funds are $100 total, shared across your allocations.\r\n\r\nVerified: 66 automated tests, desktop/mobile workflows, extracted-package startup, and three concurrent live-pr'b'ice paper settlements.\r\n\r\nReal-money trading remains disabled. Repeated floor restarts can increase cumulative losses beyond the original allowance.\r\n\r\nIs it feasible to bundle all pending versions into a single release in order to jump to version 2.2 with all change requests implemented?\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94a single BotForge27 v2.2 release is feasible. We can consolidate the remaining roadmap and change requests into one release, while still building and testing its components incrementally.\r\n\r\nHowever, this is now a substantial platform build beyond the local v1.2 demo. Completing and validating the full scope within the original 2\xe2\x80\x933-day window is not realistic.\r\n\r\nWorkstream\tConsolidated scope\r\nTrading and bots\tBot Manager, granular configurations, training, backtesting, portable files, additional contract adapters and controlled live trading\r\nAccounts and administration\tAuthentication, MFA, profiles, employee roles, audited member access, tasks, inbox, follow-ups and escalations\r\nCommerce\tMemberships, allowances, bot store, commissions, referrals, financial records, disputes and payouts\r\nCommunity and content\tLeaderboards, titles, creator channels, uploads/recording, external embeds and paid content\r\nExperience and operations\tLanding page, content management, responsive design, test accounts, monitoring and recovery\r\n\r\nThe main architectural change\r\n\r\nThe current local application cannot independently provide shared memberships'b', employee messaging, marketplace purchases and creator subscriptions across devices.\r\n\r\nThe consolidated platform needs:\r\n\r\nHosted services for accounts, permissions, public assets, billing, messages and administrative records.\r\nA local execution/storage component to preserve your private-bot and trading-data requirements.\r\nExternal integrations for authentication, email, payments, payouts, video delivery and Deriv.\r\n\r\nIt can feel like one product, but it will have multiple coordinated components.\r\n\r\nMy recommended release approach\r\n\r\nFreeze the v2.2 scope and consolidate the approved requirements into one specification.\r\nBuild working modules in dependency order, integrating them continuously.\r\nDeliver v2.2 release candidates for your testing, with simulated services clearly distinguished from genuine integrations.\r\nPublish v2.2 stable after the agreed features and integrations pass acceptance.\r\n\r\nThis avoids requiring you to install every intermediate numbered version while preserving meaningful testing.\r\n\r\nDecisions still needed before full production completion\r\n\r\nMembership prices and bot resale/derivative licences.\r\nPayment/payout providers, supported countries, fees, refund rules and approval thresholds.\r\nCreator revenue split and clarification of guest paid-channel subscriptions.\r\nHosting, media limits and remaining operational policies.\r\n\r\nThese decisions need not prevent development of the underlying modules.\r\n\r'b"\nMy recommendation: consolidate the target into v2.2, retain v1.2 as the testing baseline, and use staged release candidates rather than one untested final delivery. A delivery estimate should follow the consolidated architecture and integration assessment.\r\n\r\nwalk me through the decisions I need to make, with explanations and clickable options where possible, then let's finish the v2.2 build. Once done, let's set realistic timelines for each deliverable.\r\n\r\nYour selections are recorded, and v2.2 development has started.\r\n\r\nYou chose Windows-first local trading, test integrations, a Jamaica-based launch, an invitation-only pilot, verified accounts for content purchases, licensed bot resale, isolated Python scripts, and two-person financial approvals.\r\n\r\nThe initial policy foundation passes 85 automated tests. The complete v2.2 platform remains unfinished; accounts, training, commerce, creator channels, and staff workflows still require implementation and integration.\r\n\r\nFor one experienced full-time developer:\r\n\r\nMilestone\tInitial estimate\r\nEarlier local/demo checkpoint\t6\xe2\x80\x9310 engineering weeks\r\nComplete approved scope\t116\xe2\x80\x93185 engineering days\r\nFull release including pilot and contingency\tApproximately 6\xe2\x80\x9310 calendar months, plus external delays\r\n\r\nThese are planning estimates, to be revised after the first integrated milestone.\r\n\r\nDecisions and per-deliverable timeline\r\nDevelopment foundation source\r\nUpdated implementa"b'tion tracker\r\n\r\nThe next implementation milestone is secure accounts and local-companion pairing, followed by the Bot Manager and private-data workflows.\r\n\r\nBotForge27-v2.2-decisions-and-delivery-plan.md\r\nDocument\r\nderiv-implementation-tracker.md\r\nDocument\r\nbuild v2.2 and then create a rigorous clickable checklist for me to review, requiring approval decision and feedback for each requirement.\r\n\r\nCreated a runnable v2.2 local review candidate and a 185-item clickable checklist.\r\n\r\nThe full v2.2 release remains unfinished: 105 requirements have local test implementations, 45 are partial, and 35 are blocked. Real trading and payments remain disabled.\r\n\r\nDownload the app\r\nDownload the clickable checklist\r\nRead the build report and outstanding requirements\r\n\r\nFor Windows, extract the ZIP, run START_V22_WINDOWS.bat, and open http://127.0.0.1:8877. Python 3.11+ is required.\r\n\r\nTest login: free@test.invalid\r\nPassword: Forge27-Test-Only!\r\n\r\nClick Requirement review. Every item requires a decision, feedback, observed evidence, and environment. Nothing is pre-approved. Export your review as JSON for tracking.\r\n\r\nValidation: 121 automated tests, browser checks, and clean-package startup passed. Native Windows execution remains unverified.\r\n\r\nBotForge27-v2.2-review.html\r\nHTML\r\nBotForge27-v2.2-build-report.md\r\nDocument\r\nSaturday 7:42 PM\r\nIf i want to change the business logo or name, can that be done via the content management section 'b"of the app?\r\n\r\nPartly, in the current build.\r\n\r\nGo to Admin Console \xe2\x86\x92 Company content:\r\n\r\nBranding item\tCurrent support\r\nBusiness name\tEditable; updates the landing-page welcome text\r\nTagline, description and contact details\tEditable\r\nBusiness logo\tUpload/replacement is not implemented yet\r\nName/logo throughout the entire app\tNot fully connected; sidebar branding remains fixed\r\n\r\nContent Editors can save drafts; Full Admins can publish.\r\n\r\nFull name-and-logo management is already within the approved CMS scope, but remains incomplete in this review candidate.\r\n\r\nhow do i open the sqlite database\r\n\r\nStop the app with Ctrl+C, then copy the database as a backup before opening it.\r\n\r\nYour databases are inside the extracted app folder:\r\n\r\nData\tDatabase path\r\nAccounts, settings, test purchases and checklist reviews\tpilot-data/platform.sqlite3\r\nEach account\xe2\x80\x99s sandbox trading journal\tpilot-data/private/<account-id>/sandbox.sqlite3\r\nPublic-feed demo data\tInside pilot-public-data/ instead of pilot-data/\r\n\r\nYou can open them using a desktop SQLite database viewer: choose Open Database, select the .sqlite3 file, then browse its tables.\r\n\r\nSince Python is already required for the app, you can also inspect one without installing anything. Open a terminal in the app folder and run:\r\n\r\npython -m sqlite3 pilot-data/platform.sqlite3\r\n\r\nThen list the tables:\r\n\r\nSELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table';\r\n\r\nFor example, inspect b"b'asic account information:\r\n\r\nSELECT id, username, role, plan, status FROM users;\r\n\r\nAvoid manually changing balances, floors, orders or permissions\xe2\x80\x94those changes can break the app\xe2\x80\x99s protections and accounting.\r\n\r\ni want to view datasets as tables in vs code\r\n\r\nYes\xe2\x80\x94you can browse the app\xe2\x80\x99s SQLite datasets as tables inside VS Code.\r\n\r\nOpen Extensions with Ctrl+Shift+X.\r\nSearch for SQLite Viewer and install SQLite Viewer by Florian Klampfer.\r\nOpen your extracted BotForge27 folder in VS Code.\r\nIn the Explorer, click a database:\r\npilot-data/platform.sqlite3 \xe2\x80\x94 accounts, settings, reviews and business records.\r\npilot-data/private/<account-id>/sandbox.sqlite3 \xe2\x80\x94 trading data.\r\nFor public-feed sessions, look inside pilot-public-data/.\r\nSelect a table in the viewer to browse its rows and columns.\r\n\r\nStart with these tables:\r\n\r\nTable\tContents\r\nusers\tTest accounts and roles\r\ntrades\tRetained trading records\r\nrecords\tBots, configurations and other application records; details are in the JSON body column\r\nreviews\tChecklist decisions and feedback\r\naudit\tRecorded actions\r\nquota\tMonthly day usage\r\n\r\nUse the viewer\xe2\x80\x99s read-only mode to inspect the data without changing it.'